{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n \\\"DISPATCH POLICY|def command_ready|def command_authorize|derived_state|needs_replan|needs_revalidation|gdd|conformance_surfaces|human_approval|completion_gates\\\" Pipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py Pipeline/TaskGraph/execution_authority.py Pipeline/TaskGraph/current_conformance.py Pipeline/TaskGraph/conformance_records.py | sed -n '1,260p'; sed -n '1,260p' Tasks/NSC-003.yaml; sed -n '1,240p' Tasks/NSC-023.yaml; sed -n '1,220p' Pipeline/TaskExecution/README.md; sed -n '1,260p' Pipeline/AgentRuntime/README.md\"","aggregated_output":"Pipeline/TaskGraph/conformance_records.py:161:        \"canon\", \"validated_state\", \"conformance_surfaces\", \"gate_results\",\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/conformance_records.py:162:        \"human_approval\", \"recorded_at\",\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/conformance_records.py:207:    surfaces = record[\"conformance_surfaces\"]\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/conformance_records.py:209:        raise ConformanceRecordError(f\"{path}.conformance_surfaces must be a list.\")\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/conformance_records.py:212:        surface = _object(raw, f\"{path}.conformance_surfaces[{index}]\", {\"path\", \"blob_sha\", \"role\"})\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/conformance_records.py:213:        item_path = safe_repository_path(surface[\"path\"], f\"{path}.conformance_surfaces[{index}].path\")\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/conformance_records.py:217:        _sha(surface[\"blob_sha\"], f\"{path}.conformance_surfaces[{index}].blob_sha\")\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/conformance_records.py:218:        _text(surface[\"role\"], f\"{path}.conformance_surfaces[{index}].role\")\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/conformance_records.py:240:    approval = _object(record[\"human_approval\"], f\"{path}.human_approval\", {\"required\", \"decision\", \"approved_by\", \"notes\"})\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/conformance_records.py:242:        raise ConformanceRecordError(f\"{path}.human_approval.required must be boolean.\")\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/conformance_records.py:244:        raise ConformanceRecordError(f\"{path}.human_approval.decision is unsupported.\")\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/conformance_records.py:245:    _text(approval[\"approved_by\"], f\"{path}.human_approval.approved_by\", allow_empty=True)\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/conformance_records.py:246:    _text(approval[\"notes\"], f\"{path}.human_approval.notes\", allow_empty=True)\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/conformance_records.py:263:        if revalidation[\"reason_type\"] not in {\"code_change\", \"gdd_change\", \"contract_change\", \"periodic\", \"manual\"}:\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/current_conformance.py:111:    gates = task.get(\"completion_gates\")\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/current_conformance.py:113:        raise ConformanceRecordError(\"Current task contract completion_gates must be a list.\")\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/current_conformance.py:192:            for surface in data[\"conformance_surfaces\"]:\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/current_conformance.py:229:        approval = data[\"human_approval\"]\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/current_conformance.py:234:            invalid.append(_finding(\"human_approval_contradictory\", \"Non-required approval must use not_required.\", record))\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/current_conformance.py:253:        (\"needs_replan\", replan, \"contract_changed\", \"Current contract revision or semantic hash differs from prior evidence.\"),\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/current_conformance.py:254:        (\"needs_human\", human, \"human_approval_missing\", \"Required human approval is missing.\"),\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/current_conformance.py:255:        (\"needs_revalidation\", stale, \"evidence_stale\", \"Prior evidence exists but is not current for HEAD.\"),\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py:165:        _print_requirement_list(\"Completion gates\", task.get(\"completion_gates\", []), \"gate_id\")\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py:211:def command_ready(graph) -> int:\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py:213:        print(\"TASK READINESS: UNAVAILABLE — DISPATCH POLICY NOT ENABLED\")\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py:232:def command_authorize(graph, selector: str) -> int:\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py:248:    print(f\"derived_state:    {result.state}\")\r\n{\r\n  \"schema_version\": \"2.0\",\r\n  \"id\": \"NSC-003\",\r\n  \"contract_revision\": 1,\r\n  \"contract_disposition\": \"active\",\r\n  \"title\": \"Mouse-Directed Player Movement, Shared Pointer Projection, and Movement Restriction\",\r\n  \"reconciliation_key\": \"player-movement\",\r\n  \"kind\": \"implementation\",\r\n  \"type\": \"implementation\",\r\n  \"execution_scope\": \"single_agent\",\r\n  \"execution_reason\": \"All required responsibilities are cohesively owned by one system (PlayerMovement.cs) plus its Input Actions and builder/scene wiring; it is a bounded rewrite of one component's input/locomotion model rather than a multi-system integration.\",\r\n  \"decomposition_state\": \"concrete\",\r\n  \"decomposition_reason\": \"The required behavior (mouse-directed movement via Input System, shared pointer projection, movement-restriction interface, reset entry point) is fully specified by current GDD text without inventing new design.\",\r\n  \"parent\": \"NSC-002\",\r\n  \"depends_on\": [],\r\n  \"exclusive_resources\": [\r\n    \"repo-file:Assets/InputSystem_Actions.inputactions\",\r\n    \"repo-file:Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs\",\r\n    \"unity-scene:Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity\"\r\n  ],\r\n  \"acceptance_criteria\": [\r\n    {\r\n      \"reference\": \"GDD §2 Move and Aim\",\r\n      \"requirement\": \"Movement is mouse-directed: a click sets a destination the wizard walks toward, and holding continues steering toward the current cursor position, consumed through Unity Input System/Input Actions rather than direct hardware polling.\",\r\n      \"criterion_id\": \"AC-001\"\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"reference\": \"GDD §2 Player Movement ownership\",\r\n      \"requirement\": \"Exposes a shared world-space pointer target produced by projecting the cursor onto the gameplay plane, consumable by cursor-aimed spells and Door/Interaction without those systems independently projecting screen coordinates.\",\r\n      \"criterion_id\": \"AC-002\"\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"reference\": \"GDD §2 Player Movement ownership; §4 Development Agent Ownership Invariants\",\r\n      \"requirement\": \"Exposes an owner-controlled movement-restriction request/release interface that Charged Fireball can use to restrict movement while charging without Fireball mutating movement internals directly.\",\r\n      \"criterion_id\": \"AC-003\"\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"reference\": \"GDD §2 Floor-run restart ownership\",\r\n      \"requirement\": \"Exposes an owner-controlled reset entry point that restores player position/movement state to the floor's initial state, consumed by the Floor Run/Restart Orchestrator rather than having position mutated externally.\",\r\n      \"criterion_id\": \"AC-004\"\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"reference\": \"GDD §2 Victory/input-shutdown ownership; §4 Development Agent Ownership Invariants and final escape/victory coordination\",\r\n      \"requirement\": \"Exposes an owner-controlled gameplay-enable/suspend interface, consumed by the Game Flow/Victory capability, that immediately stops or cancels any in-progress input-driven movement (including an active click-to-destination approach), rejects new movement commands while suspended, and can be re-enabled only through an authorized reset/test flow rather than external mutation of movement internals.\",\r\n      \"criterion_id\": \"AC-005\"\r\n    }\r\n  ],\r\n  \"completion_gates\": [\r\n    {\r\n      \"reference\": \"GDD §2 Move and Aim\",\r\n      \"requirement\": \"Play Mode validation that clicking a location moves the wizard toward it and that holding steers continuously toward the live cursor position.\",\r\n      \"gate_id\": \"VAL-001\"\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"reference\": \"Victory/input-shutdown ownership\",\r\n      \"requirement\": \"Play Mode check: invoking the suspend interface while movement is active immediately halts the wizard's in-progress movement (including an active click-to-destination approach), and further movement input has no effect until an authorized re-enable/reset flow runs.\",\r\n      \"gate_id\": \"VAL-002\"\r\n    }\r\n  ],\r\n  \"downstream_integration_obligations\": [\r\n    {\r\n      \"reference\": \"GDD §2 Player Movement ownership\",\r\n      \"requirement\": \"Validate that the exposed world-space pointer target is correctly consumable by a cursor-aimed spell or Door/Interaction integration once those systems exist.\",\r\n      \"obligation_id\": \"INT-001\"\r\n    }\r\n  ],\r\n  \"gdd_evidence\": [\r\n    {\r\n      \"reference\": \"GDD §2 Player Actions and Systems — Move and Aim\",\r\n      \"requirement\": \"Mouse-directed movement through the shared Unity Input System/Input Actions layer: click to set a destination or hold to keep steering toward the cursor; Player Movement owns the shared cursor-to-gameplay-plane projection and exposes the resulting world-space pointer target consumed by movement, cursor-aimed spells, and cursor-targeted interactions (Force Wave is the exception).\"\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"reference\": \"GDD §2 Resources, Feedback, and Failure — Player Movement ownership\",\r\n      \"requirement\": \"Player Movement owns position, locomotion, movement-restriction state, and the shared cursor-to-gameplay-plane projection; runtime input is routed through Input System/Input Actions rather than direct hardware polling; Charged Fireball requests/releases its movement restriction through an owner-controlled Player Movement interface without mutating movement internals.\"\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"reference\": \"GDD §2 Loss — Floor-run restart ownership\",\r\n      \"requirement\": \"Player Movement/player position is a required reset participant invoked by the Floor Run/Restart Orchestrator through an owner-controlled reset entry point.\"\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"reference\": \"Section 2 — Victory/input-shutdown ownership bullet\",\r\n      \"requirement\": \"Player Movement, Door/Interaction, Fireball, Frost Field, and Force Wave each expose an owner-controlled gameplay-enable/suspend interface that can immediately stop or cancel their current input-driven activity, reject new gameplay commands while suspended, and be re-enabled by an authorized reset/test flow.\"\r\n    }\r\n  ],\r\n  \"basis\": \"direct_gdd\",\r\n  \"source_scope\": \"required\",\r\n  \"confidence\": \"high\",\r\n  \"notes\": \"Exact movement speed/feel tuning is left to playtesting per GDD and is not part of this item's required acceptance surface.\",\r\n  \"repository_state_at_bootstrap\": \"partial\",\r\n  \"repository_evidence_at_bootstrap\": [\r\n    {\r\n      \"path\": \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scripts/PlayerMovement.cs\",\r\n      \"evidence_type\": \"code\",\r\n      \"observation\": \"Implements only CharacterController-driven WASD movement via direct Keyboard.current polling; no mouse-directed movement, cursor projection, movement-restriction interface, or reset entry point exist.\"\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"path\": \"Assets/InputSystem_Actions.inputactions\",\r\n      \"evidence_type\": \"project_setting\",\r\n      \"observation\": \"Unmodified stock Unity template action asset; Player map has only WASD/Dpad Move and button actions (Attack/Interact/Crouch/Jump/etc.), no cursor-to-gameplay-plane or click-to-move action, and PlayerMovement.cs does not reference it.\"\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"path\": \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity\",\r\n      \"evidence_type\": \"scene\",\r\n      \"observation\": \"PlayerMovement.cs (guid 356044e6782dd0641a7e69b0abfa5c4a) is serialized on the Player GameObject, confirming current WASD movement is the integrated state, not merely available via the builder.\"\r\n    }\r\n  ],\r\n  \"provenance\": {\r\n    \"origin\": \"verified_reconciliation_bootstrap\",\r\n    \"source_schema_version\": \"1.0\",\r\n    \"reconciliation_run_id\": \"20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5\",\r\n    \"verification_run_id\": \"20260821T195959Z-43dba5de\",\r\n    \"bootstrap_status_observation\": \"open\",\r\n    \"migration_id\": \"task-contract-schema-v2-20260822-r2\"\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n{\r\n  \"schema_version\": \"2.0\",\r\n  \"id\": \"NSC-023\",\r\n  \"contract_revision\": 1,\r\n  \"contract_disposition\": \"active\",\r\n  \"title\": \"Fixed Isometric Camera\",\r\n  \"reconciliation_key\": \"fixed-isometric-camera\",\r\n  \"kind\": \"implementation\",\r\n  \"type\": \"world-foundation\",\r\n  \"execution_scope\": \"single_agent\",\r\n  \"execution_reason\": \"The fixed-camera behavior is a bounded implementation contract. Historical delivery does not make its execution scope not-applicable; current completion is derived elsewhere.\",\r\n  \"decomposition_state\": \"concrete\",\r\n  \"decomposition_reason\": \"The fixed projection/no-free-rotation behavior is a concrete bounded contract. Delivery state is intentionally separate from decomposition state.\",\r\n  \"parent\": \"NSC-022\",\r\n  \"depends_on\": [],\r\n  \"exclusive_resources\": [],\r\n  \"acceptance_criteria\": [\r\n    {\r\n      \"reference\": \"GDD - Executive Summary / 2.5D Isometric Visual and World Representation\",\r\n      \"requirement\": \"Camera renders the gameplay world using a fixed orthographic 2.5D isometric projection at a consistent dimetric angle.\",\r\n      \"criterion_id\": \"AC-001\"\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"reference\": \"GDD - 2.5D Isometric Visual and World Representation\",\r\n      \"requirement\": \"No player-controllable free rotation of the world view is exposed; the camera's rotation never changes at runtime.\",\r\n      \"criterion_id\": \"AC-002\"\r\n    }\r\n  ],\r\n  \"completion_gates\": [\r\n    {\r\n      \"reference\": \"Bootstrap repository evidence — DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests\",\r\n      \"requirement\": \"Unity validation confirms the canonical camera is orthographic and uses the fixed approved isometric rotation without a runtime rotation controller.\",\r\n      \"gate_id\": \"VAL-001\"\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"reference\": \"Bootstrap repository evidence — IsometricCameraFollow validation\",\r\n      \"requirement\": \"Unity validation confirms target-follow translation does not change the camera's fixed rotation.\",\r\n      \"gate_id\": \"VAL-002\"\r\n    }\r\n  ],\r\n  \"downstream_integration_obligations\": [\r\n    {\r\n      \"reference\": \"GDD - Camera completion and future integration\",\r\n      \"requirement\": \"Once the Tilemap/SpriteRenderer world-visual foundation exists, validate that the fixed isometric camera framing remains visually compatible with the new visual layer (alignment/sorting check), without reopening the already-satisfied fixed-angle/no-rotation requirement.\",\r\n      \"obligation_id\": \"INT-001\"\r\n    }\r\n  ],\r\n  \"gdd_evidence\": [\r\n    {\r\n      \"reference\": \"GDD - Executive Summary\",\r\n      \"requirement\": \"The camera is fixed, the world is viewed at an angle, and the visible environment is authored as isometric art rather than a free-rotation 3D space.\"\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"reference\": \"GDD - 2.5D Isometric Visual and World Representation\",\r\n      \"requirement\": \"The camera is fixed in an isometric presentation; the player does not rotate the world view freely.\"\r\n    }\r\n  ],\r\n  \"basis\": \"direct_gdd\",\r\n  \"source_scope\": \"required\",\r\n  \"confidence\": \"high\",\r\n  \"notes\": \"Per current provenance boundary: only the fixed-angle/no-free-rotation requirement is treated as GDD-backed acceptance criteria. The camera's player-follow translation (IsometricCameraFollow) is a valid current implementation detail and is recorded as repository evidence only, since the current GDD does not require player-follow camera behavior.\",\r\n  \"repository_state_at_bootstrap\": \"implemented\",\r\n  \"repository_evidence_at_bootstrap\": [\r\n    {\r\n      \"path\": \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs\",\r\n      \"evidence_type\": \"code\",\r\n      \"observation\": \"BuildCamera sets camera.orthographic = true, camera.orthographicSize = 8f, and cameraObject.transform.rotation = Quaternion.Euler(30f, -45f, 0f) once; no later code path in the builder modifies camera rotation.\"\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"path\": \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scripts/IsometricCameraFollow.cs\",\r\n      \"evidence_type\": \"code\",\r\n      \"observation\": \"LateUpdate only writes transform.position = target.position + offset; rotation is never read or written after Initialize captures the position offset.\"\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"path\": \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity\",\r\n      \"evidence_type\": \"scene\",\r\n      \"observation\": \"Serialized Main Camera has orthographic: 1, orthographic size: 8, and a wired IsometricCameraFollow component (target + offset {10,10,-10}); no rotation-modifying component is attached.\"\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"path\": \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs\",\r\n      \"evidence_type\": \"test\",\r\n      \"observation\": \"Build_MainCamera_IsFixedOrthographicIsometric asserts orthographic mode and rotation equal to Quaternion.Euler(30,-45,0) within 0.01 degrees and absence of any Rotate/Orbit-named component; Build_MainCamera_TranslatesWithPlayerButRotationStaysFixed asserts rotation is unchanged after the player moves and LateUpdate runs.\"\r\n    }\r\n  ],\r\n  \"provenance\": {\r\n    \"origin\": \"verified_reconciliation_bootstrap\",\r\n    \"source_schema_version\": \"1.0\",\r\n    \"reconciliation_run_id\": \"20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5\",\r\n    \"verification_run_id\": \"20260821T195959Z-43dba5de\",\r\n    \"bootstrap_status_observation\": \"complete\",\r\n    \"migration_id\": \"task-contract-schema-v2-20260822-r2\"\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n# TaskExecution\n\n`TaskExecution` owns No Safe Circle task identity while delegating one bounded provider-neutral invocation to `AgentRuntime`. `TaskExecutionRequest` composes an exact `AgentInvocationRequest` with `task_id` and `TaskContractIdentity`; it validates the `NSC-###` form, positive contract revision, lowercase SHA-256, and exact `Tasks/<task_id>.yaml` path.\n\n`TaskExecutionRunner` publishes immutable `task_request.json` audit data under its caller-supplied task-run root before delegating the exact contained invocation to `AgentRunner`. The generic runtime independently publishes task-neutral `request.json`, `provider.log`, and `result.json` under its configured invocation root.\n\nThese artifacts bind task identity to an invocation for audit. They do not establish delivery, readiness, conformance, authorization, approval, integration, evidence publication, or completion. ExecutionCrew orchestration, task selection, Unity execution, GER, and Git operations remain outside this layer.\n# Provider-neutral AgentRuntime\n\nThis runtime foundation extracts the reusable orchestration ideas demonstrated by Assignment 3: fresh bounded invocations, explicit request/result handoffs, semantic capability limits, structured output, normalized failures, and immutable run artifacts. `AgentCrew/` and `Assignment6GER/` remain unchanged historical coursework evidence; this package neither imports nor retrofits them.\n\n`AgentInvocationRequest` describes one generic bounded invocation: run identity, role, prompt, repository context, semantic capabilities, write policy, output shape, capability-class selection, budgets, and provider configuration. It has no No Safe Circle task identity and no dependency on `Tasks/*.yaml`. `AgentResult` describes only an invocation outcome and provider/agent claims. Task identity belongs to `Pipeline/TaskExecution`; delivery, conformance, readiness, authorization, integration, repository changes, command execution, and test success remain decisions for higher deterministic repository, test, and evidence systems.\n\nAll retained request, result, and runtime-configuration JSON values are recursively validated and frozen. Runtime configuration invariants apply equally to direct construction, dictionaries, and strict JSON loading. Only exact built-in JSON null, booleans, integers, finite numbers, valid UTF-8 strings, lists/tuples, dictionaries, and the runtime's own frozen mapping proxies are accepted. Polymorphic container and text subclasses are rejected so provider callbacks cannot run during validation or make publication disagree with the validated snapshot. Cycles and nesting deeper than the conservative 64-container limit are rejected deterministically. `to_dict()` methods return detached mutable copies. The runner accepts only the exact `AgentInvocationRequest`, `RuntimeConfiguration`, and `ProviderInvocationResponse` types; requests additionally require exact `WriteBoundaries` and `Budgets`, while results require exact `Usage`. Base serializers are called non-polymorphically, preventing subclass serialization or response-container overrides at these trust boundaries.\n\nIdentifiers use conservative lowercase ASCII forms: run IDs are 1–64 character lowercase alphanumeric/hyphen slugs, while roles and configuration keys begin with a lowercase letter and use lowercase letters, digits, underscores, and separated hyphens. Every serialized operational string must be UTF-8 encodable. Repository paths are already-normalized, relative POSIX paths and additionally reject surrogates, NUL, ASCII controls, DEL, line breaks, tabs, Windows-forbidden characters, alternate-data-stream colons, trailing dots/spaces, and reserved Windows device names. Write-boundary components are compared conservatively and case-insensitively while remaining component-aware; denied boundaries override allowed ones. Budget maxima are 1,000 turns, 86,400 seconds, and 10,000,000 tokens.\n\nProvider adapters expose an identifier, accept the common request plus a configured concrete model, return structured output and raw log text separately, and normalize provider failures. They translate invocation mechanics only. They may never write task/evidence/readiness state or declare delivery, conformance, readiness, or authorization. Live-provider adapters must pass the same contract suite.\n\n`ProviderOutputInvalid` represents provider output from which an adapter could not produce a structured-output candidate. `AgentRunner` normalizes it to `schema_error`. This is distinct from a successfully parsed candidate that the runner later rejects through central schema validation, although both outcomes normalize to `schema_error`. `ProviderRequestRejected` represents a provider that cannot safely honor a selected request policy and normalizes to `invalid_request` after provider/model selection. Exact provider raw output is retained in `provider.log` when it is valid. Neither exception establishes deterministic correctness, delivery, conformance, readiness, authorization, approval, integration, or test-success authority.\n\n`FakeProvider` is deterministic and performs no network, command, version-control, engine, or repository access. It exercises success, provider failure, invalid provider output, timeout, permission denial, budget exhaustion, and centrally detected schema failure.\n\n`ClaudeCodeProvider` now includes **Stage 4B.2 — Practical Repository Read/Search**. It supports the empty capability set, `repository_read`, `repository_search`, and their combination. Empty-capability invocations retain the Stage 4B behavior: a new empty temporary directory outside the repository with no tools. Repository-capable invocations run against the actual source repository root (`/workspace` in Docker): `repository_read` exposes native `Read`, while `repository_search` exposes native `Glob` and `Grep`. Repository write, approved command execution, and every non-null token limit remain rejected before launch.\n\nNonempty `context_paths` remain invalid without a repository capability. With a repository capability they are accepted as validated repository-relative invocation hints and added to a generated repository-context section in the effective provider prompt without mutating the `AgentInvocationRequest`. They are guidance, not a hardened read allowlist. This trusted single-user boundary intentionally prioritizes useful access to the current working tree; ADR-038 records the accepted limited risk.\n\nClaude receives the exact concrete configured model with no alias or fallback, native `--max-turns` equal to the request's required positive `turn_limit`, and the independent external hard `timeout_seconds` enforced by the shared subprocess transport. The prompt travels through stdin, while the compact output schema, exact `--tools` value, and explicit `Bash`, `Edit`, `Write`, `NotebookEdit`, `WebSearch`, and `WebFetch` denial travel as direct argv elements with `shell=False`; no `--allowedTools` argument is emitted. The shared standard-library transport captures stdout and stderr concurrently through `communicate`, creates a new process group/session, terminates the group on timeout with bounded cleanup, and preserves available partial output. Deterministic adapter and AgentRunner integration tests inject a fake process runner and never launch Claude. The opt-in `tests/claude_repository_read_search_live_smoke.py` requires `NSC_RUN_CLAUDE_REPOSITORY_SMOKE=1`; normal deterministic runs skip live Claude access.\n\nClaude stdout is decoded strictly as UTF-8 and retained exactly as `provider.log`. A successful response must be one JSON result envelope with exact built-in status metadata: `type=result`, `is_error=false`, `subtype=success`, `terminal_reason=completed`, and a present `structured_output`; `stop_reason=tool_use` is acceptable. Missing or malformed status or permission-denial metadata is `ProviderOutputInvalid`. A nonempty, exact-list `permission_denials`, other well-formed unsuccessful result envelopes, and nonzero exits are provider failures. `ProviderTransportError` covers local process/transcript failures, invalid usage metadata, and unexpected stderr on a zero exit, and `AgentRunner` normalizes it to `internal_error` while retaining any valid exact raw log. Timeouts remain `timeout` and retain valid partial stdout.\n\nNormalized Claude usage prefers all `modelUsage` entries, including cache-read and cache-creation input tokens, and otherwise falls back conservatively to top-level `usage`. An explicitly present empty `modelUsage` object and malformed, negative, boolean, non-finite, or inconsistent usage fail closed as transport errors.\n\nEach caller-supplied run root receives a uniquely and atomically created `<run_id>/` containing canonical `request.json`, `provider.log`, and `result.json`. JSON artifacts use strict finite JSON, deterministic key ordering, UTF-8 bytes, LF line endings, and one trailing LF. `provider.log` is the provider's exact raw text and is not decorated with runtime diagnostics. Each file is written and fsynced as a temporary file, then atomically hard-linked into place only if its destination does not exist; failed publication cleans its temporary file and cannot overwrite existing bytes. Existing run directories are never reused. A run directory is an audit artifact, not evidence of correctness.\n\nAfter a valid request exists, the runner creates the run directory and publishes `request.json` before resolving provider configuration. Unknown configurations, unavailable configured providers, and missing capability mappings therefore produce a preserved failed `result.json` classified as `invalid_request` without invoking a provider. Provider and model are null only when this pre-invocation selection did not succeed; post-selection failures retain the selected values.\n\nEvery failed `AgentResult` has a non-whitespace, non-authoritative `failure_message` and must keep `structured_output` null so rejected output cannot appear accepted; successful results leave the diagnostic null or empty. This diagnostic preserves configuration, provider-exception, schema-validation, and rejected-metadata details. It never establishes delivery, conformance, readiness, authorization, test success, or approval. Provider invocation failures and structured-output validation are normalized separately: unexpected ordinary provider exceptions are `internal_error`, while invalid structured JSON or schema mismatch is `schema_error`. Provider responses are field-snapshotted exactly once after their exact-type check. Structured output is immediately detached and frozen, and that same snapshot is both schema-validated and retained. Valid usage and execution/change/test claims survive a post-invocation `schema_error`, while rejected structured output remains null. Invalid raw logs are replaced by an empty log and a safe diagnostic; malformed exception text cannot block artifact publication. Process-control exceptions are not swallowed.\n\nStructured output supports only these JSON Schema features: the object, array, string, integer, number, boolean, and null types; `properties`; `required`; boolean `additionalProperties`; `items`; `enum`; and finite numeric `minimum`/`maximum` on integer or number schemas. Unknown keywords fail closed. The runner—not an adapter—validates successful output.\n\n`OpenAICodexProvider` supports empty capability, repository read, repository search, and combined read/search requests. Repository access requires an explicit externally enforced read-only construction profile; empty-capability requests use a temporary directory outside the repository. It forwards the selected concrete model, sends prompts by stdin, preserves exact JSONL stdout, parses the final-output file, uses the last completed-turn usage event, and enforces `min(timeout_seconds, turn_limit * 30)`. ArchitectureReview supplies the external Docker read-only profile. Repository writes, approved command execution, non-null token limits, fallback, and production role orchestration remain unsupported.\n\nThe runtime still has no crew roles/prompts, engine execution, GER integration, task selection, version-control workflow, TaskGraph evidence creation, conformance/readiness derivation, or dispatch authorization.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
