{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"find Pipeline -path 'Pipeline/ArchitectureReview/outputs' -prune -o -path 'Pipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence' -prune -o -type f -print | rg -i 'scanner|context|decompos|artifact|supervisor|executioncrew|ger|dispatch|claim|worktree|github|impact'; rg -n 'class .*Scanner|context pack|ContextPack|Progressive|Artifact Authority|impact analysis|dependency readiness|GitHub|worktree|Unity' Pipeline --glob '\"'!ArchitectureReview/**'\"' --glob '\"'!Reconciliation/outputs/**'\"' | sed -n '1,360p'\"","aggregated_output":"Pipeline/TaskGraph/evidence/NSC-023/artifacts/CommittedSceneCameraConformanceTests-86af98f41ab5.xml\nPipeline/TaskGraph/evidence/NSC-023/artifacts/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests-86af98f41ab5.xml\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/README.md:7:1. Is the architecture fundamentally sound for autonomous Unity game development?\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/README.md:33:- Unity Production Engineer\nPipeline/GDDRAG/README.md:166:| Windows build and canonical scene registration | nsc-gdd-039 | Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration | 325-328 | 56.62 |\r\nPipeline/GDDRAG/README.md:200:the task context pack, the project scanner, the Progressive Decomposer, Artifact Authority,\r\nPipeline/Testing/testing_policy_smoke_test.py:2:\"\"\"Static, deterministic checks for the canonical Unity testing safeguards.\"\"\"\r\nPipeline/Testing/testing_policy_smoke_test.py:36:        \"Unity asset identity\",\r\nPipeline/Testing/testing_policy_smoke_test.py:53:    require(policy, r\"move scenes, prefabs, and other Unity assets together with their `?\\.meta`? files\", \"Asset/meta move rule missing\")\r\nPipeline/Testing/testing_policy_smoke_test.py:61:    require(start_here, rf\"Unity tests.+{policy_path}\", \"START_HERE does not route Unity test work to the policy\")\r\nPipeline/Testing/testing_policy_smoke_test.py:65:        raise AssertionError(\"Runner does not check Git status both before and after Unity\")\r\nPipeline/Testing/testing_policy_smoke_test.py:66:    require(runner, r\"preHead\\s*=.+rev-parse.+HEAD\", \"Runner does not record HEAD before Unity\")\r\nPipeline/Testing/testing_policy_smoke_test.py:67:    require(runner, r\"postHead\\s*=.+rev-parse.+HEAD\", \"Runner does not record HEAD after Unity\")\r\nPipeline/Testing/testing_policy_smoke_test.py:68:    require(runner, r\"preTree\\s*=.+rev-parse.+HEAD\\^\\{tree\\}\", \"Runner does not record the Git tree before Unity\")\r\nPipeline/Testing/testing_policy_smoke_test.py:69:    require(runner, r\"postTree\\s*=.+rev-parse.+HEAD\\^\\{tree\\}\", \"Runner does not record the Git tree after Unity\")\r\nPipeline/Testing/testing_policy_smoke_test.py:73:        require(runner, re.escape(argument), f\"Unity invocation is missing {argument}\")\r\nPipeline/Testing/testing_policy_smoke_test.py:75:        raise AssertionError(\"Runner must not invoke Unity with -quit\")\r\nPipeline/Testing/testing_policy_smoke_test.py:76:    require(runner, r\"Start-Process.+-Wait.+-PassThru\", \"Runner does not explicitly wait for the Unity process\")\r\nPipeline/Testing/testing_policy_smoke_test.py:79:        raise AssertionError(\"Runner must not use LASTEXITCODE as Unity's process exit code\")\r\nPipeline/Testing/testing_policy_smoke_test.py:81:        require(runner, rf\"ConvertTo-WindowsCommandLineArgument\\s+\\${value}\", f\"Runner does not quote {value} for Unity\")\r\nPipeline/Testing/testing_policy_smoke_test.py:101:    print(\"PASS: Unity testing policy and clean-runner safeguards are present.\")\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/verification_smoke_test.py:118:                        \"requirement\": \"Install a required Unity package\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/verification_smoke_test.py:128:                        \"requirement\": \"Do not concurrently modify one Unity asset\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/verification_smoke_test.py:132:                        \"mapped_non_code_titles\": [\"No concurrent Unity asset edits\"],\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/README.md:45:## Why this is separate from the Progressive Decomposer\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/README.md:49:The Progressive Decomposer is **local and just-in-time**. It expands one near-frontier feature after Milestone 1 exists.\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/README.md:55:It does **not** invent the missing design and does **not** create an artifact proposal. Artifact proposals belong to the Progressive Decomposer in Milestone 2.\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/README.md:182:A task can be `decomposition_state: concrete` while still being `execution_scope: needs_execution_decomposition`. That means the design is known, but the implementation node bundles too much work for one safe handoff. A future Progressive Decomposer may split the implementation work without inventing new game design.\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/README.md:219:- no concurrent edits to one Unity asset -> `pipeline_constraint`.\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/README.md:261:Planning / Progressive Decomposition step may add more exact file/scene/prefab\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/streaming_refinement_identity_order_smoke_test.py:21:        \"What concrete Unity mechanism should door click-selection use, and does it \"\r\nPipeline/Testing/scene_location_smoke_test.py:2:\"\"\"Static checks for the authoritative DoorPrototype Unity scene location.\"\"\"\r\nPipeline/Testing/scene_location_smoke_test.py:63:            raise AssertionError(f\"Obsolete Unity asset path still exists: {obsolete_path}\")\r\nPipeline/Testing/scene_location_smoke_test.py:89:    print(\"PASS: DoorPrototype scene location and Unity asset identity are authoritative.\")\r\nPipeline/GDDRAG/knowledge_base/No_Safe_Circle_GDD_RAG.json:197:      \"text\": \"| Action | What the player does | Purpose |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Move and Aim | Use mouse-directed movement through the shared Unity Input System/Input Actions layer: click to set a destination or hold to keep steering toward the cursor. Player Movement owns the shared cursor-to-gameplay-plane projection and exposes the resulting world-space pointer target. Movement, cursor-aimed spells, and cursor-targeted interactions consume that shared target; Force Wave is the player-centered exception. | Create and preserve escape routes while maintaining the spatial feel of early isometric action/RPG movement and one consistent pointer projection across gameplay systems. |\\n| Fireball | Aim with the cursor. Tap for a quick, mobile shot against a single or separated enemy. Hold to charge: costs more mana and restricts movement further, rewarding preparation by damaging multiple clustered enemies. | Rewards distance and preparation; a charge's area advantage matters against groups, not against one target. |\\n| Frost Field | Place a temporary area at the current cursor world-space target that heavily slows enemies. Frost Field consumes the shared pointer target exposed by Player Movement rather than projecting screen coordinates independently. | Divides crowds and protects routes. |\\n| Force Wave | Use a player-centered short-range radial knockback with a long cooldown. Each cast costs **25 mana**. Force Wave does not use cursor direction or target selection. | Creates emergency space at a real resource cost. The 25-mana cost is an initial tuning value and may change during later balance work. |\\n| Open Sealed Door | Click the intended sealed door to request movement to its interaction position. The wizard walks there automatically. When the wizard reaches arm's-reach interaction range, the five-second opening timer begins automatically; no sustained button hold is required. Cursor drift does not matter after selection. Taking damage, moving away, or issuing another command that cancels/replaces the door approach resets the attempt. If uninterrupted for five seconds, the door opens. | Makes each exit a positioning challenge while allowing one click to mean \\\"go to this door and start opening it when I arrive.\\\" |\\n| Close and Lock | After the wizard crosses to the forward side of an opened door, the door automatically closes and locks; no additional input is required. Completing the automatic close-and-lock restores a small, fixed amount of health. Once locked, this door cannot be reopened or crossed again (see Door and Pursuit Rules). | Creates recovery time while pursuers attack it, removes an unnecessary second interaction, and marks forward progress as final. |\",\r\nPipeline/GDDRAG/knowledge_base/No_Safe_Circle_GDD_RAG.json:246:      \"text\": \"- Health: The wizard survives a few isolated hits but dies quickly when trapped. Health does not regenerate during a room, and does not reset between rooms — it carries across the whole run. Successfully locking a door after crossing restores a small, fixed amount of health before the next room begins; this recovery is partial, so accumulated damage still matters across all five rooms. Exact recovery values will be set during playtesting.\\n- Player Health ownership: The shared Player Health system is the single owner of the wizard's current health. It exposes owner-controlled damage and restore interfaces plus an observable zero-health/death transition consumed by the Floor Run/Restart Orchestrator. Door locking requests the fixed recovery through the Player Health restore interface; restoration is clamped to maximum health, and Door and Interaction never writes player-health state directly. The restart orchestrator consumes the zero-health transition rather than polling or mutating Player Health internals.\\n- Health feedback: The wizard's current health is continuously visible through a simple player-facing health indicator. Because damage persists across rooms, the player must be able to read accumulated damage and remaining survivability throughout the run.\\n- Mana: Mana regenerates slowly after a brief post-cast delay. Waiting restores power while locked doors continue losing durability. Force Wave currently costs **25 mana per cast**; this is an initial balance value that may be tuned later after the game is playable.\\n- Player Mana ownership: The shared Player Mana system is the single owner of current mana and the post-cast regeneration-delay state. Spells spend mana through its owner-controlled spend interface. Player Mana does not own spell-local cooldown, charge, cast, placement, or active-effect state merely because those spells consume mana.\\n- Movement: Circling or retreating creates temporary separation from pursuers, but Melee Enemies gradually close the distance over time; movement alone cannot preserve a completely safe distance indefinitely in later encounters. Sustaining real separation requires turning routes, obstacles, Frost Field, Force Wave, or reducing the threat directly.\\n- Player Movement ownership: The player movement system owns player position, locomotion, movement-restriction state, and the shared cursor-to-gameplay-plane projection used to produce a world-space pointer target. Runtime input is routed through the project's Unity Input System/Input Actions layer rather than independent direct hardware polling in gameplay systems. Player Movement, cursor-aimed Wizard Combat abilities, and Door/Interaction consume the shared pointer target instead of independently projecting screen coordinates. Charged Fireball requests and releases its charging movement restriction through an owner-controlled Player Movement interface; Fireball does not directly mutate movement internals.\",\r\nPipeline/GDDRAG/knowledge_base/No_Safe_Circle_GDD_RAG.json:441:      \"text\": \"- Enemies move between rooms through open doors; crossing a doorway does not clear pursuit. Active pursuit is lost only through the distance-and-search behavior defined in **Enemy Detection, Pursuit, and Target Loss**.\\n- A sealed door is a cursor-targeted interactable. Clicking the intended door issues a combined approach-and-interact request: the wizard moves to that door's interaction position, and the five-second opening timer begins automatically when the wizard reaches arm's-reach range. No sustained button hold is required. After the door is selected, cursor drift does not cancel the request or the timer. Taking damage, moving away after timing begins, or issuing another command that cancels/replaces the door interaction resets the attempt.\\n- The **Door and Interaction system owns doorway-crossing state**. After a door is open, it detects when the wizard has actually crossed to that door's forward side and exposes that state to consumers. Opening a door does not by itself count as crossing. Door locking and the final escape condition consume this same crossing state rather than implementing separate crossing detectors.\\n- **Door passability contract:** Door and Interaction owns the semantic door state (`sealed`, `open`, `locked`, `broken`). The shared gameplay navigation/locomotion layer owns translating that semantic state into enemy walkability through a shared passability interface. Sealed and locked doors block enemy traversal; open and broken doors permit forward enemy traversal. Door state changes update the navigation layer through that interface, while enemy pursuit/attack code does not independently manipulate NavMesh or doorway passability. The exact Unity mechanism used beneath this interface (for example obstacle/carving, a navigation link, or runtime navigation-data update) is an implementation choice rather than a separate game-design decision.\\n- After the shared doorway-crossing state confirms that the wizard reached the forward side, the door automatically closes and locks; the player does not provide a second close/lock input. The completed automatic lock requests the small fixed health restoration through Player Health. Any surviving enemy that is still actively tracking/pursuing the player and whose route to the player is blocked by that locked door begins attacking the door. No separate `witnessed escape` or line-of-sight-to-the-crossing state is tracked: if the enemy can still track the player, it is already aggroed/pursuing. An enemy that has already lost the player does not begin attacking a locked door solely because it is nearby. When the door breaks, those tracking/pursuing enemies continue their pursuit through the now-passable doorway. Once locked, a door cannot be reopened, unlocked, or crossed again by the player — the floor is a forward-only escape sequence.\",\r\nPipeline/GDDRAG/knowledge_base/No_Safe_Circle_GDD_RAG.json:536:      \"text\": \"- The game uses a fixed 2.5D isometric presentation inspired by Diablo 1 and Ultima Online.\\n- The primary environment visual layer uses **Unity Isometric Tilemaps** for floors, walls, and repeatable architectural tiles.\\n- The wizard, enemies, taller props, interactive doors, obstacles, decorative set pieces, and other independently sorted objects use **world-space SpriteRenderers and prefabs** instead of being painted directly into a Tilemap. Placeholder character sprites are acceptable during development, but the wizard and enemy visual representations follow the same isometric sorting conventions as other world-space SpriteRenderers.\\n- The visible isometric art layer is kept separate from the underlying gameplay representation so art can be revised without redefining core gameplay rules.\\n- The gameplay layer still owns walkability, collision, trigger volumes, door state, pursuit logic, and other simulation behavior. The reusable Tilemap/SpriteRenderer world foundation establishes authoring conventions and visual/gameplay separation; it does **not** by itself include authoring all five room layouts or encounter content.\",\r\nPipeline/GDDRAG/knowledge_base/No_Safe_Circle_GDD_RAG.json:604:      \"text\": \"Development agents help plan, implement, review, and test No Safe Circle; they do not run in the finished game. Each agent owns a visible game feature, receives concrete acceptance criteria, and produces a Unity test or implementation result.\",\r\nPipeline/GDDRAG/knowledge_base/No_Safe_Circle_GDD_RAG.json:627:      \"text\": \"| Agent | Plain-English role | Effect on this game |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Feature Planning Agent | Turns one approved feature into acceptance criteria and a file list. | Keeps prompts specific and prevents scope growth. |\\n| Wizard Combat Agent | Implements player movement, the shared Unity Input System/Input Actions consumption pattern, the Player Movement-owned cursor-to-gameplay-plane targeting projection, Fireball, Frost Field's casting, mana cost, and feedback, Force Wave, health, mana, cooldowns, and recovery. Frost Field's actual slowdown effect is applied and restored by the Enemy Pursuit Agent, which owns enemy movement and all Ranged Enemy attack behavior; this agent only triggers the Frost Field effect and does not implement Ranged Enemy targeting or attacks. | Creates the tools used to slow, attack, escape, and recover, without editing enemy-movement or enemy-attack code directly. |\\n| Door and Interaction Agent | Implements cursor-targeted door selection, click-to-approach movement request consumption, arm's-reach arrival validation, automatic five-second opening timing, interruption, shared doorway-crossing state, automatic closing/locking after forward-side crossing, runtime door durability, the owner-controlled door-damage receive interface, and breaking. It is the single owner of detecting when the wizard crosses to the forward side of an opened door; locking and final escape consume that state. Enemy attack behavior requests door damage through the Door-owned interface instead of writing durability or lifecycle state directly. After a door is clicked, cursor movement off the selected door does not cancel the approach or timer; after timing begins, damage, moving away, or issuing another command that cancels/replaces the interaction resets it. No sustained interaction hold is required. | Makes room exits risky and safety temporary without requiring sustained pointer precision after selection, while preventing multiple systems from inventing separate doorway-crossing or door-durability ownership. |\\n| Enemy Pursuit Agent | Implements detection, melee and ranged attacks, pursuit, movement through open doors, distance-based target loss, last-known-position search, bounded randomized search/wander, and reacquisition, including applying and restoring Frost Field's slowdown effect. Detection Distance is smaller than Lose Target Distance; crossing a doorway does not itself clear pursuit. Enemy attacks consume the shared Player Health damage interface rather than owning separate player-health state. Owns the reusable Enemy Health/Defeat capability for persistent enemy objects: damage sources consume its damage interface, defeat reports removal through the Active Enemy Registry interface, and floor restart consumes its reset interface. Owns Ranged Enemy targeting, attack timing, and line-of-sight/projectile-occlusion checks, so Chapel of Ash's cover actually blocks shots. Enemy locomotion consumes the shared gameplay navigation/locomotion layer rather than choosing or configuring that layer independently. Owns the shared Active Enemy Registry bookkeeping for persistent active enemy objects; encounter activation consumes that registry rather than maintaining a separate count. Owns the ongoing state of persistent enemy objects whether they are actively pursuing, searching, or later reacquired. It also owns the application of forced enemy displacement requested by abilities such as Force Wave, including returning affected enemies to the appropriate pursuit/search movement state afterward. Qualifying locked-door attacks are initiated by this enemy behavior but apply durability loss only through the Door and Interaction damage interface. | Makes enemies left alive remain a visible, persistent consequence while preserving explicit ownership of enemy health/defeat, player-health damage, navigation, registry bookkeeping, and Door-owned durability. |\\n| Dungeon Encounter Agent | Authors placements, triggers, configured per-door durability values, and final-room pressure, including the mixed Melee/Ranged compositions in Chapel of Ash and the Final Room. It does not own runtime door durability state, damage intake, or the locked-to-broken transition; those remain Door and Interaction responsibilities. Owns encounter-admission policy and consumes the shared Active Enemy Registry before activating new enemies. When persistent enemies and a requested new encounter would together exceed fifteen active enemies, this agent delays or reduces the new encounter's enemies first rather than removing existing pursuers. | Controls when the player can lure, fight, flee, or become trapped, and authors encounter/durability tuning without taking ownership of runtime Door state or the registry's persistent-enemy bookkeeping. |\\n| Unity Validation Agent | Reviews changes and creates Play Mode checks for cleanup, references, and edge cases, including Bone Archive lane pathing, Chapel of Ash occlusion, Lower Vault enemy-cap priority, target-loss hysteresis/search and reacquisition, cursor-targeted door range and cursor-drift behavior, isometric sprite sorting, and alignment between Tilemap visuals and gameplay geometry. | Prevents permanent slowdown, stuck enemies, incorrect pursuit/search transitions, inaccessible door interaction behavior, incorrect door states, and visual/gameplay desynchronization. |\",\r\nPipeline/GDDRAG/knowledge_base/No_Safe_Circle_GDD_RAG.json:679:      \"text\": \"- **Door and Interaction Agent** owns door targeting, click-to-approach interaction consumption, automatic five-second timing on arrival, shared doorway-crossing state, runtime door durability, the owner-controlled door-damage receive interface, and semantic door lifecycle state including the locked-to-broken transition. Other systems consume doorway-crossing state rather than implementing their own crossing detector, and enemy attack behavior requests durability loss through the Door-owned damage interface rather than mutating Door state directly. Door state changes are published through the shared navigation/locomotion passability interface; Door and Interaction does not own the navigation technology that translates those states into enemy walkability.\\n- **Final escape/victory coordination** owns only the won-state transition, gameplay-suspension coordination, and victory presentation. Player Movement, Door/Interaction, Fireball, Frost Field, and Force Wave remain owners of their own active behavior and must expose owner-controlled suspend/re-enable entry points consumed by victory/reset flows. Final escape logic must not reach into movement, door, or spell internals to stop them.\\n- **Door/navigation integration prerequisite:** the navigation-owned shared passability interface must exist before door-state publication through that interface is dispatch-ready. Door lifecycle work that does not require navigation may be decomposed and implemented earlier, but if passability publication remains bundled inside one executable door-lifecycle item, that item depends on the gameplay navigation/locomotion owner. An exclusive-resource lock is not a substitute for this behavioral dependency.\\n- **Dungeon Encounter Agent** owns encounter content/activation policy and consumes the shared Active Enemy Registry when admitting new enemies; it does not replace or remove existing persistent pursuers to satisfy the cap.\\n- **Unity Validation Agent** verifies integrated behavior and ownership boundaries but does not redefine runtime ownership or silently implement missing gameplay responsibilities.\\n- **Player Experience Success Criteria are required validation obligations, not advisory prose.** Each criterion in Section 3 must be represented as a validation requirement on the work item or items that own the underlying behavior. They do not require separate gameplay features when the behavior is already owned.\\n- An agent must not bypass another agent's owned runtime interface merely because both systems interact. Shared-write conflicts are sequencing/locking concerns, not proof of a dependency unless one task actually requires behavior another task must create first.\",\r\nPipeline/GDDRAG/knowledge_base/No_Safe_Circle_GDD_RAG.json:702:      \"text\": \"1. The developer approves one or more feature briefs describing what the player should see and do.\\n2. The Feature Planning Agent divides each feature into tasks, identifies dependencies, selects the required files, and removes unapproved additions.\\n3. The orchestration pipeline may assign independent tasks to multiple agents at the same time. For example, the Wizard Combat Agent can implement a spell while the Enemy Pursuit Agent works on enemy movement and the Dungeon Encounter Agent prepares an encounter layout.\\n4. Tasks that depend on unfinished work, or that require changes to the same scripts, scenes, or prefabs, are completed sequentially. Agents do not modify the same Unity assets concurrently.\\n5. Each implementation is reviewed by the Unity Validation Agent. The developer then tests the feature in Play Mode and merges the accepted change into the main project.\",\r\nPipeline/GDDRAG/knowledge_base/No_Safe_Circle_GDD_RAG.json:725:      \"text\": \"- **Minimal-context dispatch is a required pipeline constraint:** agents receive only the approved feature brief, its acceptance criteria, the relevant GDD rules, and the files plus scene/prefab information required for the active task. Unrelated project files or broad project context are not included unless the task genuinely requires them.\\n- Independent agents work in isolated branches or workspaces so their changes can be reviewed before integration.\\n- **Current prototype scene-builder lock:** scene-authoring work that changes objects generated or maintained through `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs` must take exclusive-write locks on both that builder and `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity`. This specifically applies to doorway-crossing work that creates/configures the forward-side crossing trigger and, under the current scene-built UI approach, to final-victory work that creates/configures the **You Escaped** overlay. If a later approved architecture moves the overlay or another scene object into a separately owned runtime prefab/asset, the task may lock that actual asset instead; the task graph must reflect the implementation that exists when it is dispatched.\\n- Each task produces changed files, an implementation summary, known risks, and a Play Mode test checklist.\\n- Source control commits serve as the handoff between implementation, validation, and integration.\\n- Agents may report a risk or recommend a scope cut, but they cannot redesign the game or add features without developer approval.\\n- The human developer remains responsible for architecture, merging changes, inspecting Unity scenes and prefabs, playtesting, balance, and final integration.\",\r\nPipeline/GDDRAG/knowledge_base/No_Safe_Circle_GDD_RAG.json:748:      \"text\": \"While the Door and Interaction Agent implements click-to-approach door opening, automatic five-second timing on arrival, and automatic locking after crossing, the Enemy Pursuit Agent can independently implement melee enemies following the wizard through open doors. At the same time, the Dungeon Encounter Agent can define the enemy placement for the first encounter space.\\n\\nAfter those tasks are complete, the Unity Validation Agent checks that enemies can cross an open doorway without automatically losing the player, that distance-based target loss leads through last-known-position search before target clearing, that damage or moving away interrupts opening after arrival while cursor drift after selection does not, and that crossing automatically closes and locks the door with no second input. The developer then combines and tests the features inside Unity.\",\r\nPipeline/GDDRAG/knowledge_base/No_Safe_Circle_GDD_RAG.json:770:      \"text\": \"No Safe Circle will be developed by one developer in Unity using C#. A raw Python orchestration pipeline using Claude Code agents will coordinate specialized development agents that assist with planning, implementation, review, debugging, and test design. Independent tasks may run at the same time, but tasks that modify the same scripts, scenes, or prefabs will be completed sequentially.\\n\\nSection 4 defines the six development-agent roles and their effects on the game. This section explains how those agents will be coordinated, constrained, budgeted, and validated during development.\\n\\nThe finished Windows game will not use generative AI at runtime. Enemy behavior, spell effects, doors, damage, and pursuit will run locally through standard Unity systems. The game will require no external AI service, API key, token usage, or network connection after it is built. Development-time generative tools may be used to create isometric tiles, props, and directional sprites, but once imported they behave as ordinary Unity assets.\\n\\nThe developer approves feature briefs, resolves architecture decisions, inspects scenes and prefabs, tests game feel, balances encounters, merges changes, and decides which stretch features are accepted.\",\r\nPipeline/GDDRAG/knowledge_base/No_Safe_Circle_GDD_RAG.json:793:      \"text\": \"The orchestration pipeline may run independent tasks in parallel. For example, the Wizard Combat Agent may implement Fireball and Force Wave while the Enemy Pursuit Agent independently implements melee chasing and Ranged Enemy targeting, and the Dungeon Encounter Agent plans an encounter layout. The **Development Agent Ownership Invariants** in Section 4 are mandatory pipeline constraints, not advisory prose; planning and reconciliation must preserve them as explicit process requirements and must not collapse cross-system ownership merely to simplify a task graph.\\n\\nAgents will work in isolated branches or workspaces. Two agents will not modify the same gameplay files or Unity assets at the same time. Each completed task will produce:\\n\\n- Changed files\\n- An implementation summary\\n- Known risks or limitations\\n- A Play Mode test checklist\\n- A source-control commit for review\\n\\nSource control will serve as the handoff between implementation, validation, and final integration.\",\r\nPipeline/GDDRAG/knowledge_base/No_Safe_Circle_GDD_RAG.json:839:      \"text\": \"- The project targets a **hybrid isometric architecture**. Unity **2D Tilemap Editor** (`com.unity.2d.tilemap`) is the approved authoring package for Isometric Tilemaps used for floors, walls, and repeatable architecture. **SpriteRenderer prefabs** are used for doors, props, obstacles, the wizard, enemies, and other independently sorted or interactive objects.\\n- A generated or hand-authored visual tile does **not** automatically define gameplay behavior. The simulation layer separately defines collision, door interactions, walkability, trigger zones, and navigation.\\n- The visible isometric layer and the gameplay/navigation layer should remain decoupled wherever practical so environment art can be regenerated or replaced without rewriting room logic.\\n- A shared **gameplay navigation/locomotion layer** owns the walkable movement representation and the navigation-facing configuration used by runtime movers. It also owns the navigation-side implementation of the shared door-passability interface: semantic door state is supplied by Door and Interaction, while this layer translates sealed/open/locked/broken state into enemy walkability. Enemy pursuit/search, melee/ranged behavior, status effects, and forced displacement consume this layer instead of each selecting or configuring navigation technology or doorway passability independently.\\n- The approved enemy-navigation implementation is **Unity AI Navigation** (`com.unity.ai.navigation`) using NavMesh-based runtime movement. A minimal working navigation/locomotion layer and the approved package configuration must exist before locomotion-dependent enemy implementation is dispatched, but room-specific visual authoring does not need to be complete first.\\n- The reusable visual world foundation (Tilemap/SpriteRenderer conventions and visual/gameplay separation) is distinct from authoring the five named room layouts and encounters. Five-room visual/content authoring consumes this foundation once it exists; encounter placement/content consumes the authored room spaces and the encounter-admission/cap foundation. These are real prerequisite relationships even when the downstream content features remain deferred for future decomposition.\\n- The camera is fixed in an isometric presentation; the player does not rotate the world view freely.\\n- Mouse-directed click/hold movement is routed through Unity Input System/Input Actions and projected onto the gameplay plane by the Player Movement-owned shared cursor-targeting foundation. Movement, cursor-aimed spells, and Door/Interaction consume the same world-space pointer target, preserving smooth movement similar to early isometric action/RPG controls without independent screen-to-world projection in each gameplay system.\",\r\nPipeline/GDDRAG/knowledge_base/No_Safe_Circle_GDD_RAG.json:865:      \"text\": \"- The primary environment visual layer will use Unity **Isometric Tilemaps** through the approved Unity 2D Tilemap Editor package (`com.unity.2d.tilemap`), preferably Isometric Z as Y where useful, for floors, walls, and repeatable architectural tiles. World-space **SpriteRenderer** prefabs will be used for sealed doors, tall props, obstacles, the wizard, enemies, and other independently sorted or interactive objects. Character presentation is therefore part of the reusable visual-world foundation rather than a separate rendering architecture.\\n- The gameplay layer remains separate from the visible art layer. Walkability, collision, trigger zones, doorway logic, and other simulation rules are defined independently from the Tilemap art so generated or swapped visual assets do not automatically change gameplay behavior.\\n- Player movement uses mouse-directed click/hold navigation through the project's Unity Input System/Input Actions layer. Player Movement owns the shared cursor-to-gameplay-plane projection and exposes the resulting world-space pointer target; movement, cursor-aimed spells, and Door/Interaction consume that shared result rather than independently polling pointer hardware or projecting screen coordinates. Force Wave is the player-centered radial exception and does not use cursor direction or target selection. Clicking a sealed door requests movement to its interaction position; when arm's-reach proximity is reached, the five-second opening timer begins automatically without a sustained hold, and cursor movement off the door does not cancel the selected interaction.\\n- A shared gameplay navigation/locomotion layer provides the walkable movement representation and navigation-facing configuration consumed by enemy movement. The approved implementation uses Unity AI Navigation (`com.unity.ai.navigation`) and NavMesh-based runtime movement; enemy detection/pursuit/search logic does not choose or configure a different navigation technology independently. The same layer exposes the navigation side of the shared door-passability interface and translates Door and Interaction's semantic sealed/open/locked/broken state into enemy walkability. Once that shared navigation layer exists, finite-state components control pursuit, attacks, target loss, search, and reacquisition across the floor's spaces. Enemies acquire the player inside Detection Distance and use a larger Lose Target Distance to end exact pursuit. Exceeding Lose Target Distance transitions the enemy to the player's last known position, followed by a short bounded randomized search/wander if the player is not immediately reacquired; doorway crossing alone does not clear pursuit. Exact distance thresholds, search duration, random search weighting, and the underlying Unity mechanism used to update doorway passability are implementation/playtesting details beneath the approved shared contracts. Ranged Enemy attacks include a line-of-sight/occlusion check, and Bone Archive's lane widths are validated against enemy movement/navigation requirements so both rooms' stated tactical geometry holds in practice.\",\r\nPipeline/GDDRAG/knowledge_base/No_Safe_Circle_GDD_RAG.json:891:      \"text\": \"- Enemy movement is the authoritative owner of enemy locomotion and forced displacement. Player abilities may request changes to enemy motion but do not directly manipulate enemy position or navigation state. Force Wave determines which enemies are affected and the radial knockback to request; the enemy movement system applies that displacement, preserves valid movement/navigation state, and resumes the appropriate pursuit/search state afterward. Temporary movement modifiers such as Frost Field slowdown are applied through the enemy status-effect system and restored when the effect ends. The pursuit/search state contract must therefore exist before status-effect/displacement work is treated as independently dispatchable; status/displacement consumes that contract when handing control back to normal enemy behavior.\\n- A reusable **Enemy Health/Defeat** runtime capability owns each persistent enemy's current health, damage intake, defeat transition, and floor-restart reset. Fireball and any other canon-required enemy-damage source call that capability's damage interface. Defeat then reports removal through the Active Enemy Registry's owner-controlled interface; neither damage sources nor the restart orchestrator directly write enemy-health or registry internals.\\n- All five encounter spaces exist inside one continuous Unity scene or continuous floor representation. Enemy objects, enemy health, pursuit state, active-enemy bookkeeping, and door state persist naturally as the player advances between spaces; no scene-load or cross-scene state-transfer system is required.\\n- A reusable status-effect/displacement component will apply Frost Field slowdown, apply enemy-owned forced displacement requests, and restore each enemy to the appropriate pursuit/search movement state afterward, per the Wizard Combat Agent / Enemy Pursuit Agent ownership split defined in Section 4. It consumes the pursuit/search state contract rather than defining a second enemy-state machine.\\n- A reusable door component will control click-to-approach interaction, automatic five-second timing on arrival, interruption, shared doorway-crossing state, automatic closing/locking after forward-side crossing, runtime durability, the owner-controlled door-damage receive interface, and breaking. It records when the wizard crosses to the forward side of an opened door and exposes that state to automatic locking and final-victory logic; those consumers do not implement separate crossing detection. Enemy locked-door attack behavior requests damage through the Door-owned interface, while the door component owns durability reduction and the locked-to-broken transition. Once a door is automatically locked after crossing or broken by enemies, it does not return to an earlier state.\",\r\nPipeline/GDDRAG/knowledge_base/No_Safe_Circle_GDD_RAG.json:917:      \"text\": \"- A reusable **Game Flow/Victory** capability consumes the final door's shared forward-side crossing state, owns the won-state transition and **You Escaped** presentation, and coordinates gameplay suspension through owner-controlled interfaces. Player Movement, Door/Interaction, Fireball, Frost Field, and Force Wave must each provide a suspend/re-enable entry point that stops current input-driven activity as well as future commands. The victory capability does not directly mutate locomotion, door-interaction, spell-charge, cast, cooldown, or other owner-internal state.\\n- A shared **Floor Run/Restart Orchestrator** coordinates a new floor attempt when it receives Player Health's zero-health/death transition. It invokes reset entry points owned by each stateful system rather than directly mutating their internals. Player Health, Player Mana, Player Movement/player position, spell-local Fireball/Frost Field/Force Wave state, enemy health and pursuit/search/attack/status/displacement state, Active Enemy Registry bookkeeping, door lifecycle/crossing/durability state, and encounter activation/admission state must all participate once those systems exist. Enemy reset returns each persistent enemy to the authored encounter/spawn region associated with its original room and clears all knowledge/state from the failed run; exact placement inside that region may vary. Early staged restart implementation is valid only as an incremental step; the persistent-systems closure remains required until all implemented state owners are connected to the orchestrator.\\n- Player Mana owns current mana and the post-cast regeneration-delay state and exposes an owner-controlled spend/reset interface to spells and restart orchestration. Force Wave spends 25 mana per cast through this interface as its initial tuning value. Spell-local cooldown/charge/cast/active-field state stays on the spell that owns it. Player Movement owns position/locomotion, the shared cursor-to-gameplay-plane projection, and the owner-controlled movement-restriction interface consumed by Charged Fireball. Runtime actions are supplied through Unity Input System/Input Actions rather than independent direct hardware polling inside gameplay systems.\\n- A shared Active Enemy Registry maintains the current persistent active-enemy set, active count, and remaining capacity. Enemy activation/defeat update that registry; target loss or room transitions do not unregister a surviving enemy. The Dungeon Encounter Agent queries the registry before activating new encounter enemies and delays or reduces new activation first when the result would exceed fifteen; existing persistent enemies are never removed to create capacity.\\n- Development-time art acquisition may use approved tools that can generate isometric tiles, props, and directional sprites. Those outputs are imported into Unity as normal assets; they do not imply automatic prefab creation, collision setup, sorting configuration, or gameplay integration.\",\r\nPipeline/GDDRAG/knowledge_base/No_Safe_Circle_GDD_RAG.json:940:      \"text\": \"The project will use a maximum planning budget of 1.5 million combined input and output tokens across approximately thirty to forty development tasks. This is a spending ceiling rather than a required amount.\\n\\nMost focused tasks will be limited to approximately 15,000–30,000 tokens. Larger context will be reserved for integration, debugging, and reviewing systems that interact with several Unity components.\\n\\n| Budget category | Tokens |\\n|---|---|\\n| Feature planning and task preparation | 150,000 |\\n| Wizard combat and spells | 300,000 |\\n| Doors and enemy pursuit | 400,000 |\\n| Dungeon encounters and integration | 250,000 |\\n| Review, debugging, and validation | 400,000 |\\n| Total ceiling | 1,500,000 |\\n\\n**Failed-task retry policy is a required pipeline constraint:** If a task fails, its scope and context will be reduced before it is attempted again. The entire project will not be repeatedly submitted to an agent for a single bug.\",\r\nPipeline/GDDRAG/knowledge_base/No_Safe_Circle_GDD_RAG.json:947:      \"title\": \"Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration\",\r\nPipeline/GDDRAG/knowledge_base/No_Safe_Circle_GDD_RAG.json:949:      \"subsection\": \"Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration\",\r\nPipeline/GDDRAG/knowledge_base/No_Safe_Circle_GDD_RAG.json:953:        \"Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration\"\r\nPipeline/GDDRAG/knowledge_base/No_Safe_Circle_GDD_RAG.json:963:      \"text\": \"- **Unity 2D Tilemap Editor** (`com.unity.2d.tilemap`) is approved and required for the intended Isometric Tilemap authoring workflow. If it is absent from `Packages/manifest.json`, adding/configuring it is concrete project-configuration work, not deferred design.\\n- **Unity AI Navigation** (`com.unity.ai.navigation`) is the approved enemy-navigation implementation. If it is absent from `Packages/manifest.json`, adding/configuring it is a prerequisite for the gameplay navigation/locomotion layer and locomotion-dependent enemy work.\\n- The required delivery target is a **Windows Standalone build**. The current canonical gameplay scene is `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity`; that scene (or a later human-approved replacement canonical gameplay scene) must be registered in Unity Build Settings before the Windows delivery requirement can be considered complete. A committed `EditorBuildSettings.asset` with no registered canonical gameplay scene is confirmed incomplete build configuration and must remain represented as open configuration work. The top-level `Assets/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity` and `Assets/Scenes/SampleScene.unity` stubs do not satisfy this requirement merely because they are `.unity` files.\\n- Package-manifest and ProjectSettings changes authorized by these approved requirements may be implemented by an agent, but the developer must inspect the resulting package state, scene registration, and Windows build configuration before merge.\",\r\nPipeline/GDDRAG/knowledge_base/No_Safe_Circle_GDD_RAG.json:986:      \"text\": \"- Generative AI tools are used only during development. Approved development-time asset tools may be used to generate isometric tiles, props, and directional sprite art, but they are not runtime dependencies.\\n- API credentials will remain outside the Unity project and source control.\\n- Minimal-context dispatch is mandatory: agents receive only the approved feature brief, its acceptance criteria, relevant GDD rules, and files plus scene/prefab information required for the active task. Unrelated repository context is withheld unless the active task requires it.\\n- Agents cannot add mechanics, redesign the game, or expand the scope without developer approval.\\n- Generated C# must compile before the task moves to validation.\\n- Unity scenes, prefab references, animations, and Inspector assignments require human inspection.\\n- Agent-generated work is not merged until its Play Mode checklist has been tested.\\n- Parallel work is limited to features that do not modify the same files or depend on unfinished systems.\",\r\nPipeline/Testing/run_unity_tests_clean.ps1:11:    [string]$UnityExecutable,\r\nPipeline/Testing/run_unity_tests_clean.ps1:18:$ExitUnity = 20\r\nPipeline/Testing/run_unity_tests_clean.ps1:88:        Stop-WithCode $ExitPrecondition \"PRECONDITION FAILURE: ProjectPath is not a Unity project: $resolvedProjectPath\"\r\nPipeline/Testing/run_unity_tests_clean.ps1:106:    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($UnityExecutable)) {\r\nPipeline/Testing/run_unity_tests_clean.ps1:107:        $UnityExecutable = Join-Path ${env:ProgramFiles} \"Unity\\Hub\\Editor\\$unityVersion\\Editor\\Unity.exe\"\r\nPipeline/Testing/run_unity_tests_clean.ps1:109:    if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $UnityExecutable -PathType Leaf)) {\r\nPipeline/Testing/run_unity_tests_clean.ps1:110:        Stop-WithCode $ExitPrecondition \"PRECONDITION FAILURE: Unity executable does not exist: $UnityExecutable\"\r\nPipeline/Testing/run_unity_tests_clean.ps1:113:    $artifactDirectory = Join-Path ([System.IO.Path]::GetTempPath()) (\"NoSafeCircle-UnityTests-\" + [Guid]::NewGuid().ToString(\"N\"))\r\nPipeline/Testing/run_unity_tests_clean.ps1:132:        $unityProcess = Start-Process -FilePath $UnityExecutable -ArgumentList $unityArguments -Wait -PassThru\r\nPipeline/Testing/run_unity_tests_clean.ps1:175:        Stop-WithCode $ExitUnity \"UNITY FAILURE: Unity could not be invoked: $invocationFailure`nXML: $xmlPath`nLog: $logPath\"\r\nPipeline/Testing/run_unity_tests_clean.ps1:178:        Stop-WithCode $ExitUnity \"UNITY FAILURE: Unity exited with code $unityExitCode.`nXML: $xmlPath`nLog: $logPath\"\r\nPipeline/Testing/run_unity_tests_clean.ps1:181:        Stop-WithCode $ExitResult \"RESULT FAILURE: Unity did not create the required XML result file: $xmlPath\"\r\nPipeline/Testing/run_unity_tests_clean.ps1:215:    Write-Host \"Unity version: $unityVersion\"\r\nPipeline/Testing/run_unity_tests_clean.ps1:216:    Write-Host \"Unity executable: $UnityExecutable\"\r\nPipeline/Testing/run_unity_tests_clean.ps1:217:    Write-Host \"Unity exit code: $unityExitCode\"\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/phase1_execution_authority_smoke_test.py:41:    assert \"a conformant result does not establish dependency readiness\" in assessment.message\r\nPipeline/Testing/README.md:1:# Clean Unity Test Execution\r\nPipeline/Testing/README.md:3:`Docs/Engineering/UNITY_TESTING_POLICY.md` defines the provider-neutral safety and evidence policy. `run_unity_tests_clean.ps1` is the authoritative deterministic Windows wrapper for a clean Unity test run. They exist because test assertions alone cannot establish that a run was safe or that the repository state being reported was preserved.\r\nPipeline/Testing/README.md:7:`Assets/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity` is the sole authoritative Door Prototype scene. Its Unity asset identity is preserved by moving it together with `Assets/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity.meta`.\r\nPipeline/Testing/README.md:11:The selected task contract and current approved GDD define required behavior. The testing policy defines how it may be proven safely. Neither this directory, the runner, nor an agent's assessment is game-design canon. The runner reports deterministic Unity and Git facts; it does not create conformance, readiness, delivery, or execution authorization.\r\nPipeline/Testing/README.md:25:Using `powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File` prevents a local execution policy from blocking the committed wrapper. Use `-UnityExecutable` to override the Unity Hub-derived executable path or `-ProjectPath` to select the Unity project. The project must begin completely clean, including untracked files.\r\nPipeline/Testing/README.md:27:The runner waits for the exact Unity process to finish, captures that process's exit code, and then waits for a short bounded period for the XML result to become visible. It still performs the post-run HEAD, tree, and status checks when Unity fails or XML is absent. The runner fails if Unity changes HEAD or leaves any working-tree change, even when all assertions pass. It never restores or hides the changes, and it preserves the unique temporary artifact directory on every result.\r\nPipeline/Testing/README.md:33:XML results and the Unity log are initially written to a unique operating-system temporary directory outside the repository. This prevents the evidence mechanism itself from dirtying the checkout and preserves failed-run diagnostics for human inspection.\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/MANUAL_DELIVERY_RECORD_TEMPLATE.md:33:## Deterministic and Unity evidence\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/MANUAL_DELIVERY_RECORD_TEMPLATE.md:37:- Unity version:\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/MANUAL_DELIVERY_RECORD_TEMPLATE.md:38:- Unity compilation result:\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/prompts/verification/structure_auditor.md:45:11. Do otherwise-independent tasks that will modify the same source file, Unity scene, prefab, builder, or non-merge-safe integration surface share the same `exclusive_resources` key?\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/prompts/verification/structure_auditor.md:141:The current GDD now resolves the navigation-technology decision: Unity AI\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/prompts/verification/structure_auditor.md:166:If Progressive Decomposition later creates a concrete authored-room\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/prompts/verification/structure_auditor.md:300:eventually exist in the same Unity scene or because another task already locks\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/CONFORMANCE_RECORDS.md:131:A conformant result does not establish dependency readiness. Dependency-readiness policy and dispatch authorization policy have not been implemented or approved: `taskcontrol ready` remains unavailable, `taskcontrol authorize` remains denied with exit code `2` and reason code `evidence_derived_dispatch_policy_not_enabled`, and zero tasks may be autonomously dispatched. State inspection and a conformant result never authorize autonomous execution.\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/architecture_review_resume.py:184:        \"dirty_worktree_allowed\": bool(args.allow_dirty),\nPipeline/TaskExecution/README.md:7:These artifacts bind task identity to an invocation for audit. They do not establish delivery, readiness, conformance, authorization, approval, integration, evidence publication, or completion. ExecutionCrew orchestration, task selection, Unity execution, GER, and Git operations remain outside this layer.\nPipeline/TaskGraph/execution_authority.py:11:    \"conformant result does not establish dependency readiness. Dependency-readiness policy \"\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/task_contract_migration.py:37:            \"Unity validation confirms the canonical camera is orthographic and \"\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/task_contract_migration.py:44:            \"Unity validation confirms target-follow translation does not change \"\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/task_contract_migration.py:56:            \"Unity Input System/Input Actions rather than direct hardware polling.\"\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/task_contract_migration.py:141:            \"Door selection/approach input is consumed through the project's Unity Input \"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/prompts/verification/refiner.md:21:4. `Packages/manifest.json` when declared Unity package availability is directly relevant\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/prompts/verification/refiner.md:184:- classify `execution_scope` separately from design decomposition. If approved design is concrete but the implementation item is too broad for one bounded agent handoff, use `needs_execution_decomposition` rather than inventing subtask design. Use `human_integration_required` when the next meaningful step fundamentally requires human Unity/editor/integration judgment;\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/prompts/verification/refiner.md:185:- add, remove, or normalize `exclusive_resources` when current repository/GDD/architecture evidence establishes that otherwise-ready tasks would modify the same non-merge-safe source file, Unity scene, prefab, builder, or logical integration surface. Shared resource locks are scheduling constraints, not dependencies.\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/prompts/verification/refiner.md:488:technical architecture/configuration such as approved Unity package\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/synthesis.json:8:    \"executive_verdict\": \"The architectural kernel is sound, but the current production architecture is not. Durable repository state, bounded workers, deterministic checks, Git isolation, runtime evidence, and bounded repair are good foundations. However, stale canon can be dispatched, editable task status can unlock dependencies without merged evidence, Unity validation is outside the executable loop, and post-bootstrap graph evolution is only documented. Reorganize around one evidence-bound gameplay delivery loop; do not continue with the bundled Milestone 2 plan.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/synthesis.json:11:      \"Integrated repository state as implementation truth and humans as authority for design, game feel, risky Unity integration, and merge.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/synthesis.json:14:      \"One bounded worker in an isolated branch/worktree before attempting parallel development.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/synthesis.json:15:      \"Validation bundles in which Unity/runtime evidence can overturn static or semantic approval.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/synthesis.json:17:      \"Explicit identification of Unity conflict surfaces such as scenes, Input Actions, ProjectSettings, and the shared builder.\"\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/synthesis.json:23:      \"Replace the milestone order with gameplay-first delivery: task context, one worker, Unity validation, human experiential gate, merge, and guarded completion. Decomposition and artifact machinery should be demand-driven.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/synthesis.json:32:      \"Defer the bundled generic RAG, project scanner/cache, Roslyn analysis, Progressive Decomposer, Artifact Authority, artifact generation, and Artifact GER platform until a real near-frontier task requires them.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/synthesis.json:34:      \"Defer GitHub Projects synchronization, claims/leases, parallel workers, resource scheduling, merge queues, automatic merge, continuous scheduling, and backlog replenishment.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/synthesis.json:74:        \"issue\": \"How much Unity automation belongs in the first slice?\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/synthesis.json:76:          \"The Unity review asks for a pinned batch compile, EditMode, PlayMode, serialization, and build runner immediately.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/synthesis.json:77:          \"The producer/YAGNI reviews accept a human-operated Unity loop initially.\"\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/synthesis.json:91:    \"minimum_viable_architecture\": \"Five authorities are sufficient: current human-approved GDD/decisions; small versioned task contracts describing intent and dependencies; integrated main for implementation truth; an append-only attempt/evidence manifest; and explicit human approvals. A human selects one player-valued task. One restartable coordinator creates or resumes one worktree, pins canon/contract/base hashes, and invokes one bounded coding worker with direct targeted context. Validation runs scope/diff checks, Unity compilation, task-specific EditMode/PlayMode checks, a fresh semantic diff review, and only the necessary human scene/game-feel gate. Failures return one normalized feedback bundle through a bounded repair loop. Completion is recorded only after the candidate is integrated and matching evidence exists; readiness derives from those receipts. Git branches, checkpoint commits, manifests, and retained logs provide recovery without a workflow database, GitHub synchronization, generic RAG, or multi-worker scheduler.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/synthesis.json:94:      \"why\": \"`PlayerMovement.cs` still polls `Keyboard.current` for WASD while current canon requires Input Actions-based click-to-destination and held-pointer steering. The slice is player-visible, establishes the shared pointer boundary needed by doors and spells, and makes NSC-019 immediately ready. It also exercises the exact missing production seams: canon freshness, Input Actions, scene integration, Unity evidence, recovery, merge, and truthful completion.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/synthesis.json:97:        \"Create one isolated worktree and compact attempt manifest containing attempt ID, task/contract/canon/base hashes, allowed files, required gates, checkpoints, and evidence paths. Use direct current-GDD excerpts, not the stale RAG.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/synthesis.json:100:        \"Run Unity 6000.1.8f1 compilation plus focused EditMode/PlayMode tests through a thin host command that preserves XML/log evidence and checks for unexplained working-tree mutations. Perform one human pass for movement feel, projection accuracy, camera compatibility, and scene wiring.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/synthesis.json:104:      \"success_test\": \"From a clean checkout at canon hash H, start the attempt, restart it once after a checkpoint, and prove it resumes without duplicating work. In the canonical scene, click moves toward a fixed destination; holding follows changing cursor positions; restriction and suspension stop movement immediately; reset returns the initial movement state; and sampled screen points project consistently onto the gameplay plane. Unity compile/EditMode/PlayMode evidence and the human check pass, the candidate is merged once, and a matching receipt makes NSC-019 ready exactly once. Negative cases must prove that editing only `status: complete`, supplying a non-ancestor commit, omitting Unity evidence, or changing the task/GDD hash does not unlock NSC-019.\"\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/synthesis.json:109:      \"A freely editable `complete` status currently unlocks dependents without merge, canon, test, or Unity evidence.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/synthesis.json:111:      \"No repository-owned Unity/CI runner exists in the frozen commit, and no Unity executable was available in the review environment.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/synthesis.json:116:      \"The prior no-op rule already encouraged an unnecessary defensive code/test change to manufacture an Assets diff rather than resolve the missing Unity evidence.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/synthesis.json:119:    \"decision_rationale\": \"Independent repository evidence supports the core conclusion. The frozen HEAD is the requested commit. All TaskGraph smoke tests and `taskcontrol validate` pass, proving structural value, but `taskcontrol_smoke_test.py` deliberately changes one task to `complete` and observes downstream readiness without evidence. The CLI exposes only validate/list/show/ready/graph, and post-bootstrap reconciliation returns `taskcontrol_diff_required` with empty changes. Zero tasks contain canon hashes, requirement versions, merge SHAs, or evidence receipts. The RAG identifies a July 30 GDD and retains WASD/manual-door rules; the current GDD is dated August 21 and changed by 143 additions and 48 deletions after the RAG commit. `PlayerMovement.cs` still implements direct WASD polling, Build Settings contains no scenes, and the repository has no Unity batch/CI path. Assignment 6 supplies positive evidence for bounded repair and runtime feedback: two static camera passes failed in Unity and a no-op refinement was caught. Conversely, from the Assignment 6 commit to the frozen commit, 201 commits produced 130,935 inserted lines—mostly generated pipeline/audit material—while changing nothing under Assets, Packages, or ProjectSettings. That is strong evidence to preserve the worker/evidence kernel while replacing reconciliation-first milestone ordering.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/synthesis.json:121:      \"A functioning external Windows Unity runner, licensing setup, or CI system may exist outside the frozen repository.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/synthesis.json:125:      \"No production task has exercised Progressive Decomposition, Artifact Authority, or artifact promotion, so their eventual necessity and proper shape remain unknown.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/synthesis.json:127:      \"The review environment could not independently rerun Unity compilation, EditMode, PlayMode, or the documented final camera playtest.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:2:  \"current_state_summary\": \"The only committed canonical gameplay scene is Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity, generated/maintained by Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs. Within that scene: (1) the Main Camera is orthographic, fixed at the classic 30/-45 dimetric isometric rotation, and only ever translated (never rotated) by IsometricCameraFollow.cs, with automated editor tests (DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs) directly asserting orthographic mode, the fixed rotation, absence of any Rotate/Orbit component, and that rotation never changes while following the player — this satisfies the GDD's fixed-isometric/no-free-rotation requirement. (2) Packages/manifest.json and Packages/packages-lock.json contain neither com.unity.2d.tilemap nor com.unity.ai.navigation; only the builtin com.unity.modules.tilemap runtime module (a different, lower-level module than the 2D Tilemap Editor authoring package) is present. (3) No Tilemap/Grid GameObjects and no NavMeshAgent/NavMeshSurface/NavMeshObstacle components exist anywhere in the scene; the scene's NavMeshSettings block is Unity's default per-scene stub, not evidence of an implemented navigation layer. (4) The builder currently generates Floor, Walls, and the door visual as plain primitive meshes (Plane/Cube) with MeshRenderers rather than as Isometric Tilemap tiles or world-space SpriteRenderer prefabs, so the reusable Tilemap/SpriteRenderer visual foundation and its sorting/visual-gameplay-separation conventions do not yet exist.\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:3:  \"desired_state_summary\": \"The GDD requires: a fixed 2.5D isometric camera with no free world-view rotation; the approved Unity 2D Tilemap Editor (com.unity.2d.tilemap) and Unity AI Navigation (com.unity.ai.navigation) packages configured in the project; a shared gameplay navigation/locomotion layer that owns the walkable movement representation for NavMesh-based enemy movement and that also owns the navigation-side half of the door-passability contract (translating Door and Interaction's semantic sealed/open/locked/broken state into enemy walkability, with sealed/locked blocking and open/broken permitting traversal); and a reusable Tilemap/SpriteRenderer visual-world foundation (Isometric Tilemaps for floors/walls/repeatable architecture, world-space SpriteRenderer prefabs with isometric sorting for the wizard, enemies, doors, and other independently sorted objects) that is decoupled from the gameplay/collision/walkability layer so art can change without redefining gameplay rules, and that supports all five spaces existing inside one continuous scene/floor with no scene-loading or cross-scene state transfer. Room-specific five-space authoring and encounter content remain explicitly out of scope for this foundation and belong to a separate deferred content-authoring feature outside this domain.\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:6:    \"Neither approved package (com.unity.2d.tilemap nor com.unity.ai.navigation) appears in Packages/manifest.json or Packages/packages-lock.json. This is concrete missing project-configuration work per the GDD's 'Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration' section, not deferred design.\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:7:    \"No Tilemap/Grid objects and no NavMesh runtime components (NavMeshAgent/NavMeshSurface/NavMeshObstacle) exist anywhere in the current scene. The scene's NavMeshSettings YAML block is Unity's default per-scene stub present in every scene and is not evidence of an implemented navigation/locomotion layer.\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:8:    \"DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs currently builds Floor, Walls, and the door visual as primitive Unity meshes (Plane/Cube) with MeshRenderer, not as Isometric Tilemap tiles or world-space SpriteRenderer prefabs, so the required Tilemap/SpriteRenderer visual-world foundation does not currently exist.\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:16:      \"title\": \"World and Unity Foundations\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:25:          \"requirement\": \"Fixed 2.5D isometric presentation using Unity Isometric Tilemaps for architecture and world-space SpriteRenderer prefabs for independently sorted/interactive objects, with the gameplay layer kept separate from the visual layer.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:29:          \"requirement\": \"A shared gameplay navigation/locomotion layer owning walkable movement representation and the navigation side of the door-passability interface, using the approved Unity AI Navigation package.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:123:          \"reference\": \"GDD - Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:124:          \"requirement\": \"Unity 2D Tilemap Editor (com.unity.2d.tilemap) is approved and required for the intended Isometric Tilemap authoring workflow; if absent from Packages/manifest.json, adding/configuring it is concrete project-configuration work, not deferred design.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:127:          \"reference\": \"GDD - Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:128:          \"requirement\": \"Unity AI Navigation (com.unity.ai.navigation) is the approved enemy-navigation implementation; if absent from Packages/manifest.json, adding/configuring it is a prerequisite for the gameplay navigation/locomotion layer and locomotion-dependent enemy work.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:133:          \"reference\": \"GDD - Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:137:          \"reference\": \"GDD - Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:141:          \"reference\": \"GDD - Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:147:          \"reference\": \"GDD - Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:162:          \"observation\": \"Resolved dependency graph includes only com.unity.modules.tilemap (the builtin low-level Tilemap runtime module bundled with Unity) and no com.unity.2d.tilemap or com.unity.ai.navigation entry at any depth, confirming neither approved package is currently resolved.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:195:          \"requirement\": \"The approved implementation uses Unity AI Navigation (com.unity.ai.navigation) and NavMesh-based runtime movement; enemy detection/pursuit/search logic does not choose or configure a different navigation technology independently. The exact underlying mechanism for updating doorway passability is an implementation choice.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:213:          \"requirement\": \"Implemented using Unity AI Navigation (com.unity.ai.navigation) with NavMesh-based runtime movement as the approved technology.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:221:          \"requirement\": \"The specific Unity mechanism used beneath the passability interface (obstacle/carving, navigation link, or runtime navigation-data update) is left as an implementation choice.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:240:          \"observation\": \"No NavMeshAgent, NavMeshSurface, or NavMeshObstacle component exists anywhere in the scene; the only NavMesh-related block present is Unity's default per-scene NavMeshSettings stub (m_NavMeshData: {fileID: 0}), which every Unity scene contains by default and is not evidence of a built navigation layer.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:245:          \"observation\": \"Assembly definition references only Unity.InputSystem and UnityEngine.UI; no AI Navigation assembly reference exists.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:252:          \"evidence\": \"Packages/manifest.json and Packages/packages-lock.json currently contain no com.unity.ai.navigation entry; GDD 'Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration' states its absence is a concrete configuration prerequisite for the navigation/locomotion layer.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:273:      \"decomposition_reason\": \"The GDD already specifies the approved technology (Unity AI Navigation/NavMesh), the ownership split for the passability interface, and the exact semantic-to-walkability translation rules; no additional design invention is needed to scope this item.\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:290:          \"requirement\": \"The primary environment visual layer uses Unity Isometric Tilemaps for floors, walls, and repeatable architectural tiles; the wizard, enemies, taller props, interactive doors, obstacles, decorative set pieces, and other independently sorted objects use world-space SpriteRenderers and prefabs instead of being painted into a Tilemap.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:298:          \"requirement\": \"All five encounter spaces exist inside one continuous Unity scene or continuous floor representation; enemy objects, health, pursuit state, active-enemy bookkeeping, and door state persist naturally as the player advances; no scene-load or cross-scene state-transfer system is required.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:308:          \"requirement\": \"Floors, walls, and repeatable architectural tiles are authored using Unity Isometric Tilemaps (com.unity.2d.tilemap).\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:320:          \"requirement\": \"The world/floor representation supports all five spaces existing inside one continuous Unity scene with no scene-loading or cross-scene state transfer required.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:325:          \"reference\": \"GDD - Unity Validation Agent role (Section 4)\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:329:          \"reference\": \"GDD - Unity Validation Agent role (Section 4)\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/world_foundations.json:351:          \"evidence\": \"Packages/manifest.json and Packages/packages-lock.json currently contain no com.unity.2d.tilemap entry; GDD 'Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration' states its absence is concrete project-configuration work required for the Tilemap authoring workflow.\"\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol_smoke_test.py:36:        assert \"A conformant result does not establish dependency readiness\" in ready_output\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py:216:        print(\"A conformant result does not establish dependency readiness.\")\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py:256:    if result.dirty_worktree:\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/wizard_combat.json:3:  \"desired_state_summary\": \"The GDD requires three spells owned by the Wizard Combat Agent: Charged Fireball (cursor-aimed tap-for-quick-shot / hold-to-charge-for-area-damage against clustered Melee Enemies, consuming a Player-Movement-owned movement-restriction interface while charging and an Enemy-Health/Defeat-owned damage interface to hurt enemies), Frost Field (placed at the Player-Movement-owned shared cursor world-space target, providing readable cast/active-field feedback, and triggering — but not implementing — enemy slowdown through the Enemy Pursuit/status-effect owner), and Force Wave (player-centered radial knockback, no cursor targeting, 25-mana initial cost, long cooldown, requesting enemy displacement through the same enemy-owned status-effect/displacement interface). Each spell owns its own local state (Fireball: tap/charge/cast; Frost Field: Wizard-Combat-side cast/placement/active-field; Force Wave: cooldown) and must expose an owner-controlled floor-restart reset entry point and an owner-controlled gameplay-enable/suspend interface consumed by the victory/game-flow capability. All three spend mana through Player Mana's existing spend interface and must route casting input through Unity Input System/Input Actions rather than independent hardware polling.\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/wizard_combat.json:107:          \"requirement\": \"Fireball's casting input (tap/charge/release) is routed through the project's Unity Input System/Input Actions layer rather than independent direct hardware polling.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/wizard_combat.json:220:          \"requirement\": \"Frost Field's casting input is routed through the project's Unity Input System/Input Actions layer rather than independent direct hardware polling.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/wizard_combat.json:329:          \"requirement\": \"Force Wave's cast input is routed through the project's Unity Input System/Input Actions layer rather than independent direct hardware polling.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/prompts/verification/execution_scope_auditor.md:33:- `human_integration_required` — the next meaningful step fundamentally requires human Unity/editor/integration judgment.\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/prompts/verification/execution_scope_auditor.md:40:- work that requires simultaneous edits across unrelated owners or many Unity assets;\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/prompts/verification/execution_scope_auditor.md:50:Do not decompose the game into speculative microtasks and do not invent missing design. If a work item is too broad, report that fact and describe the boundary problem. A later Progressive Decomposer can perform the actual bounded split using approved design.\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/prompts/verification/execution_scope_auditor.md:83:The current GDD deliberately leaves the concrete Unity navigation technology as\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/prompts/verification/execution_scope_auditor.md:87:Unity-editor judgment.\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/prompts/verification/execution_scope_auditor.md:119:- enemy navigation uses Unity AI Navigation (`com.unity.ai.navigation`);\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/prompts/verification/execution_scope_auditor.md:120:- Isometric Tilemap authoring uses Unity 2D Tilemap Editor\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/prompts/verification/execution_scope_auditor.md:138:where the next meaningful step itself cannot be performed without human Unity\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/current_conformance.py:39:    dirty_worktree: bool\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:4:    \"desired_state_summary\": \"Player Core Systems: Per the current GDD (Docs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md), Player Movement must own player position/locomotion, movement-restriction state, and a shared cursor-to-gameplay-plane projection producing a world-space pointer target consumed by movement itself, cursor-aimed spells (Fireball, Frost Field), and Door/Interaction (Force Wave is the explicit player-centered exception). Movement must be mouse-directed (click to set a destination or hold to steer toward the cursor) and routed through the project's Unity Input System/Input Actions layer rather than direct hardware polling. Player Movement must expose an owner-controlled movement-restriction interface that Charged Fireball requests/releases without mutating movement internals directly, and must expose an owner-controlled reset entry point for player position/movement state consumed by the Floor Run/Restart Orchestrator. Player Health must remain the single owner of current health, exposing an owner-controlled damage interface (already present) plus an owner-controlled restore/heal interface clamped to max health (consumed by Door's lock-and-heal request), an observable zero-health/death transition consumed by the restart orchestrator without polling or internal mutation, a continuous player-facing health indicator, no passive regeneration, and an owner-controlled floor-restart reset entry point. Player Mana must remain the single owner of current mana and its post-cast regeneration-delay state, exposing the existing spend interface plus an owner-controlled reset entry point for floor restart, while never absorbing spell-local cooldown/charge/cast/placement/active-field state that belongs to Fireball, Frost Field, or Force Wave; denied-cast attempts should surface player-readable feedback given the GDD's failure-readability success criterion (exact presentation is an implementation choice). Wizard Combat and Spells: The GDD requires three spells owned by the Wizard Combat Agent: Charged Fireball (cursor-aimed tap-for-quick-shot / hold-to-charge-for-area-damage against clustered Melee Enemies, consuming a Player-Movement-owned movement-restriction interface while charging and an Enemy-Health/Defeat-owned damage interface to hurt enemies), Frost Field (placed at the Player-Movement-owned shared cursor world-space target, providing readable cast/active-field feedback, and triggering — but not implementing — enemy slowdown through the Enemy Pursuit/status-effect owner), and Force Wave (player-centered radial knockback, no cursor targeting, 25-mana initial cost, long cooldown, requesting enemy displacement through the same enemy-owned status-effect/displacement interface). Each spell owns its own local state (Fireball: tap/charge/cast; Frost Field: Wizard-Combat-side cast/placement/active-field; Force Wave: cooldown) and must expose an owner-controlled floor-restart reset entry point and an owner-controlled gameplay-enable/suspend interface consumed by the victory/game-flow capability. All three spend mana through Player Mana's existing spend interface and must route casting input through Unity Input System/Input Actions rather than independent hardware polling. Enemy State, Persistence, and Shared Effects: The GDD requires three reusable, ownership-scoped enemy capabilities beneath the Enemies feature: (1) a shared Active Enemy Registry that tracks persistent active enemy objects, exposes current count/remaining capacity under a hard 15-enemy cap, registers on activation, unregisters on defeat, and is never depleted by target loss/search/room-crossing/waiting-behind-a-locked-door; (2) a reusable Enemy Health/Defeat capability that owns each persistent enemy's current health, damage intake (consumed by Fireball and other canon damage sources), the defeat transition, and reports defeat-driven removal through the registry's own interface rather than duplicating bookkeeping; (3) a reusable status-effect/displacement component that applies and restores Frost Field slowdown (locomotion/repositioning only — it must never suppress or slow Ranged Enemy attack execution) and applies Force-Wave-requested forced displacement, returning the affected enemy to the correct pursuit/search movement state afterward. All three must expose owner-controlled reset entry points consumed by the Floor Run/Restart Orchestrator (owned by a different domain) rather than being reset by direct internal mutation. Enemy Pursuit and Attack Behavior: The GDD requires a shared detection/pursuit/target-loss/search/reacquisition state machine (Detection Distance smaller than Lose Target Distance; distance-based, non-random loss; last-known-position homing; short bounded randomized search/wander; reacquisition; no target clearing merely from crossing a doorway; explicit continued traversal through open/broken doorways) consumed by two required enemy archetypes: Melee Enemy (rushes and attacks at close range, naturally clusters while pursuing, gradually closes distance so retreat alone cannot preserve indefinite safety) and Ranged Enemy (keeps moderate distance, fires a slow telegraphed shot with line-of-sight/projectile occlusion, continues attacking while slowed by Frost Field even though its repositioning is slowed). Both archetypes deal damage only through the shared Player Health interface and must be delivered as usable assembled world-space SpriteRenderer prefabs integrating pursuit/locomotion, Enemy Health/Defeat, and Active Enemy Registry participation (both owned elsewhere). Separately, the GDD requires that any surviving enemy still actively tracking/pursuing the player and blocked by a newly locked door attacks that door (no separate witness flag) through the Door-owned damage interface, and resumes pursuit through the doorway once it breaks. Doors and Interaction: The GDD requires Door and Interaction to: (1) expose sealed doors as cursor-targeted interactables that trigger a combined approach-and-interact request through Player Movement's shared world-space pointer target, auto-start a five-second timer with no sustained hold once arm's-reach range is reached, ignore cursor drift after selection, and reset on damage/moving-away/interaction-cancelling commands; (2) own authoritative doorway-crossing state (forward-side crossing) as a single owner consumed by close/lock and final victory; (3) automatically close and lock the door after crossing (no second input), request a fixed Player Health restoration through Player Health's owner-controlled restore interface, own runtime durability and an owner-controlled damage-receive interface consumed by enemy attacks, own the locked-to-broken transition, enforce forward-only progression (no reopening/unlocking/re-crossing; a broken door stays open and is not a player return path), publish semantic door state through the navigation-owned passability interface, and provide required breach feedback (banging/shaking/cracks/durability indicator); and (4) expose an owner-controlled reset entry point for door lifecycle/crossing/durability state consumed by the Floor Run/Restart Orchestrator. World and Unity Foundations: The GDD requires: a fixed 2.5D isometric camera with no free world-view rotation; the approved Unity 2D Tilemap Editor (com.unity.2d.tilemap) and Unity AI Navigation (com.unity.ai.navigation) packages configured in the project; a shared gameplay navigation/locomotion layer that owns the walkable movement representation for NavMesh-based enemy movement and that also owns the navigation-side half of the door-passability contract (translating Door and Interaction's semantic sealed/open/locked/broken state into enemy walkability, with sealed/locked blocking and open/broken permitting traversal); and a reusable Tilemap/SpriteRenderer visual-world foundation (Isometric Tilemaps for floors/walls/repeatable architecture, world-space SpriteRenderer prefabs with isometric sorting for the wizard, enemies, doors, and other independently sorted objects) that is decoupled from the gameplay/collision/walkability layer so art can change without redefining gameplay rules, and that supports all five spaces existing inside one continuous scene/floor with no scene-loading or cross-scene state transfer. Room-specific five-space authoring and encounter content remain explicitly out of scope for this foundation and belong to a separate deferred content-authoring feature outside this domain. Floor Content and Encounters: The GDD requires one continuous handcrafted floor with five named tactical spaces (Ruined Entry, Bone Archive, Chapel of Ash, Lower Vault, Final Room), each with a specific layout/tactical purpose, built on the reusable Tilemap/SpriteRenderer world foundation. Encounter placement, triggers, per-door durability values, and mixed Melee/Ranged compositions (explicitly required in Chapel of Ash and the Final Room) are authored by the Dungeon Encounter Agent once the room spaces and admission foundation exist, but exact geometry/placement/tuning is intentionally left to later authoring/playtesting. Independently, the GDD already fully specifies a runtime encounter-admission rule that must not be blocked by deferred content: a shared Active Enemy Registry tracks up to fifteen active persistent enemies; when admitting a new encounter would exceed that cap, new encounter enemies are delayed/reduced first and existing persistent pursuers are never removed, with Lower Vault as the explicit validation case. Section 3 also fixes a three-to-eight enemy encounter-size range and the never-isolated-Ranged-Enemy composition rule as durable constraints on future encounter authoring. Run Lifecycle and Victory: The GDD requires two closely related capabilities in this domain. (1) Floor Run/Restart: a shared Floor Run/Restart Orchestrator consumes Player Health's observable zero-health/death transition and coordinates a fresh floor attempt by invoking owner-controlled reset entry points on every run-persistent state owner (Player Health, Player Mana, Player Movement/position, Fireball, Frost Field, Force Wave, enemy health/defeat, enemy pursuit/search/attack/status/displacement, Active Enemy Registry, door lifecycle/crossing/durability, encounter activation/admission). An early implementation may validate only against currently-existing persistent owners, but full closure remains required until every implemented owner participates. (2) Win/Loss Conditions: victory is confirmed only via the shared doorway-crossing state Door and Interaction owns (no separate crossing detector); a reusable Game Flow/Victory capability then owns the won-state transition, displays a simple \\\"You Escaped\\\" overlay, and coordinates suspension of Player Movement, Door/Interaction, Fireball, Frost Field, and Force Wave through each system's own owner-controlled suspend/re-enable interface rather than mutating their internals. No further post-victory progression is required. Loss is reaching zero health, which triggers the restart orchestrator described above. Delivery, Validation, and Pipeline Constraints: The GDD requires a Windows Standalone build as part of the Required Scope (Section 3) and Technical Strategy (Section 5), with the canonical gameplay scene registered in Unity Build Settings before that delivery obligation can be considered complete. The GDD also defines an extensive set of required development-process invariants governing the AI-assisted pipeline: minimal-context dispatch, isolated-branch/workspace execution with no concurrent edits to the same Unity assets, a scene-builder exclusive-write lock for `DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs`/`DoorPrototype.unity`, compile-before-validation, failed-task scope-reduction retry policy, a planning token-budget ceiling, mandatory human inspection/merge/playtesting authority, agent scope/canon discipline, preservation of non-canonical stub scenes as a human decision, a boundary on what generated development-time art import does and does not imply, credential handling outside source control, and a runtime prohibition on generative AI in the shipped game. Section 3's Player Experience Success Criteria are required validation obligations that must be attached to the concrete work items (owned by other domain workers) that actually implement the underlying behavior, not treated as separate gameplay features. Stretch goals and explicitly excluded systems must be enumerated and kept out of required Milestone 1 work.\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:5:    \"current_state_summary\": \"Player Core Systems: Current repository state for player-core systems (Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scripts/PlayerMovement.cs, PlayerHealth.cs, PlayerMana.cs, PlayerManaUI.cs, plus their scene wiring in DoorPrototype.unity and creation code in DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs): PlayerMovement.cs is a CharacterController-driven WASD keyboard mover that polls Keyboard.current directly every Update; it has no mouse-directed click/hold movement, no cursor-to-gameplay-plane projection/world-space pointer target, no movement-restriction request/release interface, and no floor-restart reset entry point. It does call PlayerInteractionController.OnPlayerMoved() on movement input, which is the one GDD-relevant behavior already present. PlayerHealth.cs exposes CurrentHealth, a clamped TakeDamage(amount) damage interface, and a Damaged event, but has no restore/heal interface, no observable zero-health/death transition, and no reset entry point; there is no PlayerHealth UI component anywhere in the scripts, so the GDD's continuous health indicator is not implemented. PlayerMana.cs exposes CurrentMana/MaxMana, Spend(amount) (fails silently returning false when insufficient), Tick-based post-cast-delay regeneration, and a ManaSpent event; PlayerManaUI.cs already renders a continuous fill-bar tied to CurrentMana/MaxMana (confirmed wired in DoorPrototype.unity via DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.BuildManaUI). PlayerMana has no reset entry point and no denied-cast feedback signal. All four scripts (PlayerHealth, PlayerMana, PlayerMovement, PlayerManaUI) are confirmed serialized onto the Player/Canvas GameObjects in Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity (verified by matching each script's .meta GUID against the scene file), and DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.BuildPlayer/BuildUI is the current write surface that creates and wires them. Assets/InputSystem_Actions.inputactions is the unmodified default Unity template (Move/Look/Attack/Interact/Crouch/Jump/etc. plus a UI map with Point/Click); it has no gameplay-plane cursor-projection or click-to-move action, and PlayerMovement.cs does not consume it at all — it bypasses the Input Actions layer entirely via raw Keyboard.current polling, which conflicts with the GDD's explicit \\\"routed through Unity Input System/Input Actions\\\" requirement. PlayerManaPlayModeTests.cs and part of DoorInteractionPlayModeTests.cs give real Play Mode test coverage for PlayerMana spend/regen/post-cast-delay behavior and for PlayerHealth's damage-cancels-door-interaction interaction, but no tests exist for movement, cursor projection, health restore, or any reset behavior. Wizard Combat and Spells: Repository inspection confirms Fireball, Frost Field, and Force Wave do not exist anywhere under Assets/ (no matches for those terms, and no dedicated scripts). Only PlayerMana.cs (mana pool + Spend/regen), PlayerHealth.cs (damage), PlayerMovement.cs (WASD-only CharacterController movement, no mouse/cursor projection, no movement-restriction interface), PlayerInteractionController.cs, and DoorInteractable.cs are integrated. No enemy scripts of any kind exist (no health/damage/defeat capability, no status-effect/displacement capability), so any spell-consumed enemy-owned interface is itself an unfinished prerequisite. Assets/InputSystem_Actions.inputactions exists with a generic Player action map (Move/Look/Attack/Interact/Crouch/Jump/Previous/Next/Sprint) but has only one combat-adjacent action (\\\"Attack\\\", a plain Button bound to Mouse leftButton with no tap/hold distinction) and no Frost-Field- or Force-Wave-specific actions, so all three spells must add/modify bindings in that shared asset. DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs assembles the Player GameObject (movement, interaction, health, mana, debug controls) and rebuilds/saves the canonical DoorPrototype.unity scene on every run, so attaching any new spell component to the Player requires modifying that builder and re-saving that scene. Enemy State, Persistence, and Shared Effects: The current integrated project contains zero enemy-related code, components, or scene objects. A repository-wide search of Assets/ (Scripts, Tests, and Editor) for Enemy, Registry, Defeat, Frost, and Displacement returned no matches. DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs creates only Floor, Walls, DoorRoot, and player/camera-related objects — no enemy scaffolding. PlayerHealth.cs exists (health/damage only, no death event or restore method) but is outside this domain's ownership. No Active Enemy Registry, Enemy Health/Defeat capability, or status-effect/displacement component exists in any form (not even a builder-capability stub). This domain's required work is therefore entirely missing/open. Enemy Pursuit and Attack Behavior: The current repository contains zero enemy-related code. `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scripts/` only has Door/Player/Camera/UI scripts (DoorInteractable.cs, DoorInteractionUI.cs, PlayerHealth.cs, PlayerInteractionController.cs, PlayerMovement.cs, PlayerMana.cs, PlayerManaUI.cs, IsometricCameraFollow.cs, DebugDamageControl.cs, DebugManaSpendControl.cs); a full `Assets/**/*.cs` glob confirms no file named Enemy/Melee/Ranged/Pursuit exists anywhere. `Packages/manifest.json` does not declare `com.unity.ai.navigation`, so the shared gameplay navigation/locomotion foundation this domain's locomotion depends on is itself unconfigured. `DoorInteractable.cs` currently implements only open/interacting/progress state — no durability, locked, or broken fields and no damage-receive interface exist yet, so the door-side interface locked-door enemy attacks must consume does not exist. `PlayerHealth.cs` already exposes a public `TakeDamage(float)` method, which is a usable existing interface for enemy attacks. Apparent \\\"NavMesh\\\"/\\\"Occlusion\\\" strings in `DoorPrototype.unity` are generic per-GameObject Unity serialization fields (`m_NavMeshLayer`, occlusion-culling settings), not an actual navigation or line-of-sight implementation. Doors and Interaction: The DoorPrototype scene implements a WASD-movement, arm's-reach-trigger, hold-E interaction model (DoorInteractable.cs + PlayerInteractionController.cs), with a five-second progress timer that resets on release/movement/damage. This is a partial precursor to the GDD's required door contract but is missing cursor-targeted click-to-approach selection, an automatic no-hold timer start, and Unity Input System/Input Actions routing (current code polls Keyboard.current directly). Nothing in the repository implements doorway-crossing detection, automatic close/lock, runtime durability, a damage-receive interface, the locked-to-broken transition, forward-only enforcement, breach feedback, or door-state publication to a navigation/passability layer — none of these exist in any script, the scene, or the builder. PlayerHealth.cs exposes only TakeDamage (no restore/heal interface), and no Unity AI Navigation package/NavMesh gameplay layer exists, confirming that this domain's cross-domain prerequisites (Player Movement's shared pointer projection, Player Health's restore interface, and the gameplay navigation/passability layer) are genuinely unfinished rather than stale ordering. World and Unity Foundations: The only committed canonical gameplay scene is Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity, generated/maintained by Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs. Within that scene: (1) the Main Camera is orthographic, fixed at the classic 30/-45 dimetric isometric rotation, and only ever translated (never rotated) by IsometricCameraFollow.cs, with automated editor tests (DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs) directly asserting orthographic mode, the fixed rotation, absence of any Rotate/Orbit component, and that rotation never changes while following the player — this satisfies the GDD's fixed-isometric/no-free-rotation requirement. (2) Packages/manifest.json and Packages/packages-lock.json contain neither com.unity.2d.tilemap nor com.unity.ai.navigation; only the builtin com.unity.modules.tilemap runtime module (a different, lower-level module than the 2D Tilemap Editor authoring package) is present. (3) No Tilemap/Grid GameObjects and no NavMeshAgent/NavMeshSurface/NavMeshObstacle components exist anywhere in the scene; the scene's NavMeshSettings block is Unity's default per-scene stub, not evidence of an implemented navigation layer. (4) The builder currently generates Floor, Walls, and the door visual as plain primitive meshes (Plane/Cube) with MeshRenderers rather than as Isometric Tilemap tiles or world-space SpriteRenderer prefabs, so the reusable Tilemap/SpriteRenderer visual foundation and its sorting/visual-gameplay-separation conventions do not yet exist. Floor Content and Encounters: The current checkout contains only the single-door prototype under Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype: a generic Floor plane, two Wall cubes, one DoorRoot/DoorInteractable, a Player object, and UI, all generated by DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs and serialized in DoorPrototype.unity. There is no Tilemap content, no named room (Ruined Entry, Bone Archive, Chapel of Ash, Lower Vault, Final Room), no encounter/spawn/trigger authoring, and no Active Enemy Registry, encounter-admission, or enemy code anywhere in Assets/ (confirmed via glob of all NoSafeCircle .cs files and a grep for Encounter/Registry terms, which returned no matches). Five-room content and dungeon encounters are therefore entirely unimplemented, matching their status as GDD-deferred authoring plus one not-yet-built runtime foundation (encounter-admission cap enforcement). Run Lifecycle and Victory: No restart, death-transition, or victory/game-flow code exists anywhere in the current repository. PlayerHealth.cs only exposes CurrentHealth, TakeDamage, and a Damaged event — there is no zero-health/death event, no restore/heal interface, and no reset entry point. PlayerMana.cs and PlayerMovement.cs likewise expose no reset entry points. DoorInteractable.cs implements only the open-timer/interaction lifecycle (Progress, IsOpen, doorwayBlocker disable-on-open) — it has no doorway-crossing detection, no lock/durability/breaking, and no reset entry point either, even though its own Progress/IsOpen/blocker state is already run-persistent. No enemy, spell (Fireball/Frost Field/Force Wave), or navigation code exists in the project at all (confirmed by a repository-wide search), so the eventual full persistent-closure and victory-input-shutdown dependencies on those systems are currently unmet prerequisites rather than already-satisfied interfaces. Delivery, Validation, and Pipeline Constraints: This worker directly inspected only the global_pipeline domain (delivery/build configuration and non-code/pipeline requirements). `ProjectSettings/EditorBuildSettings.asset` contains `m_Scenes: []` — no gameplay scene is registered in Unity Build Settings, confirming the Windows-build delivery obligation has a concrete open configuration gap. `Packages/manifest.json` does not currently declare `com.unity.2d.tilemap` or `com.unity.ai.navigation` (this fact is relevant to the world_foundations domain, not represented as a work item here). `.gitignore` confirms `UserSettings/` and other Unity editor/user-local state are excluded from source control, consistent with the GDD's process expectations. No previous-candidate delivery/pipeline work items were found to be stale; the three routed keys (`no-safe-circle`, `delivery-and-build`, `windows-build-scene-registration`) remain current and required.\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:8:      \"PlayerMovement bypasses the Unity Input System/Input Actions layer entirely (direct Keyboard.current polling) despite `using UnityEngine.InputSystem`, which conflicts with the GDD's explicit 'routed through the project's Unity Input System/Input Actions layer' requirement for both movement and door/spell input.\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:9:      \"Assets/InputSystem_Actions.inputactions is the stock Unity template asset (Move/Look/Attack/Interact/Crouch/Jump/Sprint/etc.) with no cursor-to-gameplay-plane or click-to-move action; implementing mouse-directed movement will require adding/modifying bindings in this shared asset.\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:33:      \"The apparent NavMesh/Occlusion strings inside DoorPrototype.unity are ordinary Unity per-GameObject serialization boilerplate (m_NavMeshLayer, OcclusionCullingSettings), not evidence of an actual navigation mesh, line-of-sight system, or enemy content; this negative finding is called out explicitly to avoid an overbroad claim being read as a positive one.\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:35:      \"door-open-interaction: current code (DoorInteractable.cs, PlayerInteractionController.cs) implements arm's-reach range detection via a trigger collider and a 5-second progress timer that cancels on player-health damage and on any player movement, matching the interruption/timer-progression concept, but it requires a sustained hold of the E key (GDD explicitly requires no sustained hold once the timer auto-starts on arrival) and has no cursor-targeted click-to-approach selection, no consumption of a shared world-space pointer target, and uses raw Keyboard.current polling rather than the Unity Input System/Input Actions layer. Classified partial.\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:44:      \"Neither approved package (com.unity.2d.tilemap nor com.unity.ai.navigation) appears in Packages/manifest.json or Packages/packages-lock.json. This is concrete missing project-configuration work per the GDD's 'Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration' section, not deferred design.\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:45:      \"No Tilemap/Grid objects and no NavMesh runtime components (NavMeshAgent/NavMeshSurface/NavMeshObstacle) exist anywhere in the current scene. The scene's NavMeshSettings YAML block is Unity's default per-scene stub present in every scene and is not evidence of an implemented navigation/locomotion layer.\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:46:      \"DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs currently builds Floor, Walls, and the door visual as primitive Unity meshes (Plane/Cube) with MeshRenderer, not as Isometric Tilemap tiles or world-space SpriteRenderer prefabs, so the required Tilemap/SpriteRenderer visual-world foundation does not currently exist.\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:62:      \".gitignore (approved narrow metadata source) confirms UserSettings/ and other Unity editor/user-local state are excluded from the committed repository, consistent with the GDD's process framing; no gameplay/design conclusions were drawn from it.\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:145:          \"requirement\": \"Mouse-directed movement through the shared Unity Input System/Input Actions layer: click to set a destination or hold to keep steering toward the cursor; Player Movement owns the shared cursor-to-gameplay-plane projection and exposes the resulting world-space pointer target consumed by movement, cursor-aimed spells, and cursor-targeted interactions (Force Wave is the exception).\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:159:          \"requirement\": \"Movement is mouse-directed: a click sets a destination the wizard walks toward, and holding continues steering toward the current cursor position, consumed through Unity Input System/Input Actions rather than direct hardware polling.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:195:          \"observation\": \"Unmodified stock Unity template action asset; Player map has only WASD/Dpad Move and button actions (Attack/Interact/Crouch/Jump/etc.), no cursor-to-gameplay-plane or click-to-move action, and PlayerMovement.cs does not reference it.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:208:          \"evidence\": \"GDD §2 Move and Aim requires routing through Unity Input System/Input Actions; current InputSystem_Actions.inputactions has no click-to-move or cursor-projection action for the Player map.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:497:          \"requirement\": \"Fireball's casting input (tap/charge/release) is routed through the project's Unity Input System/Input Actions layer rather than independent direct hardware polling.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:610:          \"requirement\": \"Frost Field's casting input is routed through the project's Unity Input System/Input Actions layer rather than independent direct hardware polling.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:719:          \"requirement\": \"Force Wave's cast input is routed through the project's Unity Input System/Input Actions layer rather than independent direct hardware polling.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:1039:          \"requirement\": \"Verify Frost Field slowdown applies to a pursuing enemy's movement speed and is fully restored when the effect ends, with no permanent slowdown remaining (Unity Validation Agent concern in Section 4).\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:1145:          \"reference\": \"Development Agent Roles (Unity Validation Agent)\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:1335:          \"reference\": \"Dungeon Floor Structure (Chapel of Ash) / Development Agent Roles (Unity Validation Agent)\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:1519:          \"requirement\": \"Movement, cursor-aimed spells, and Door/Interaction consume the shared world-space pointer target exposed by Player Movement rather than independently projecting screen coordinates; runtime input is routed through Unity Input System/Input Actions rather than direct hardware polling.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:1541:          \"requirement\": \"Door selection/approach input is consumed through the project's Unity Input System/Input Actions layer rather than independent direct hardware polling.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:1550:          \"reference\": \"Section 4 — Unity Validation Agent row; Example Game-Specific Agent Task\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:1573:          \"observation\": \"BeginInteraction/EndInteraction are driven by a keyboard hold of interactKey (default Key.E) read directly from Keyboard.current in Update(), not by a cursor click-to-approach request or the Unity Input System/Input Actions layer; EndInteraction is also called on any player movement and on PlayerHealth.Damaged, matching the GDD's interruption rules for movement/damage.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:1706:      \"decomposition_reason\": \"The GDD specifies the exact required behavior (detect actual forward-side crossing after open, expose to consumers, participate in reset); the underlying Unity mechanism is an explicit implementation choice, not missing design.\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:1869:      \"title\": \"World and Unity Foundations\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:1878:          \"requirement\": \"Fixed 2.5D isometric presentation using Unity Isometric Tilemaps for architecture and world-space SpriteRenderer prefabs for independently sorted/interactive objects, with the gameplay layer kept separate from the visual layer.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:1882:          \"requirement\": \"A shared gameplay navigation/locomotion layer owning walkable movement representation and the navigation side of the door-passability interface, using the approved Unity AI Navigation package.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:1976:          \"reference\": \"GDD - Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:1977:          \"requirement\": \"Unity 2D Tilemap Editor (com.unity.2d.tilemap) is approved and required for the intended Isometric Tilemap authoring workflow; if absent from Packages/manifest.json, adding/configuring it is concrete project-configuration work, not deferred design.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:1980:          \"reference\": \"GDD - Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:1981:          \"requirement\": \"Unity AI Navigation (com.unity.ai.navigation) is the approved enemy-navigation implementation; if absent from Packages/manifest.json, adding/configuring it is a prerequisite for the gameplay navigation/locomotion layer and locomotion-dependent enemy work.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:1986:          \"reference\": \"GDD - Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:1990:          \"reference\": \"GDD - Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:1994:          \"reference\": \"GDD - Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2000:          \"reference\": \"GDD - Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2015:          \"observation\": \"Resolved dependency graph includes only com.unity.modules.tilemap (the builtin low-level Tilemap runtime module bundled with Unity) and no com.unity.2d.tilemap or com.unity.ai.navigation entry at any depth, confirming neither approved package is currently resolved.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2048:          \"requirement\": \"The approved implementation uses Unity AI Navigation (com.unity.ai.navigation) and NavMesh-based runtime movement; enemy detection/pursuit/search logic does not choose or configure a different navigation technology independently. The exact underlying mechanism for updating doorway passability is an implementation choice.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2066:          \"requirement\": \"Implemented using Unity AI Navigation (com.unity.ai.navigation) with NavMesh-based runtime movement as the approved technology.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2074:          \"requirement\": \"The specific Unity mechanism used beneath the passability interface (obstacle/carving, navigation link, or runtime navigation-data update) is left as an implementation choice.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2093:          \"observation\": \"No NavMeshAgent, NavMeshSurface, or NavMeshObstacle component exists anywhere in the scene; the only NavMesh-related block present is Unity's default per-scene NavMeshSettings stub (m_NavMeshData: {fileID: 0}), which every Unity scene contains by default and is not evidence of a built navigation layer.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2098:          \"observation\": \"Assembly definition references only Unity.InputSystem and UnityEngine.UI; no AI Navigation assembly reference exists.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2105:          \"evidence\": \"Packages/manifest.json and Packages/packages-lock.json currently contain no com.unity.ai.navigation entry; GDD 'Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration' states its absence is a concrete configuration prerequisite for the navigation/locomotion layer.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2126:      \"decomposition_reason\": \"The GDD already specifies the approved technology (Unity AI Navigation/NavMesh), the ownership split for the passability interface, and the exact semantic-to-walkability translation rules; no additional design invention is needed to scope this item.\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2143:          \"requirement\": \"The primary environment visual layer uses Unity Isometric Tilemaps for floors, walls, and repeatable architectural tiles; the wizard, enemies, taller props, interactive doors, obstacles, decorative set pieces, and other independently sorted objects use world-space SpriteRenderers and prefabs instead of being painted into a Tilemap.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2151:          \"requirement\": \"All five encounter spaces exist inside one continuous Unity scene or continuous floor representation; enemy objects, health, pursuit state, active-enemy bookkeeping, and door state persist naturally as the player advances; no scene-load or cross-scene state-transfer system is required.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2161:          \"requirement\": \"Floors, walls, and repeatable architectural tiles are authored using Unity Isometric Tilemaps (com.unity.2d.tilemap).\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2173:          \"requirement\": \"The world/floor representation supports all five spaces existing inside one continuous Unity scene with no scene-loading or cross-scene state transfer required.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2178:          \"reference\": \"GDD - Unity Validation Agent role (Section 4)\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2182:          \"reference\": \"GDD - Unity Validation Agent role (Section 4)\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2204:          \"evidence\": \"Packages/manifest.json and Packages/packages-lock.json currently contain no com.unity.2d.tilemap entry; GDD 'Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration' states its absence is concrete project-configuration work required for the Tilemap authoring workflow.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2345:          \"requirement\": \"All five encounter spaces exist inside one continuous Unity scene or continuous floor representation; no scene-load or cross-scene state-transfer system is required.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2355:          \"requirement\": \"All five spaces are authored inside one continuous Unity scene/floor representation rather than separate scenes with cross-scene state transfer.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2371:      \"decomposition_reason\": \"Exact room geometry, dimensions, prop placement, and chokepoint/cover layout for each of the five named spaces are not specified beyond their stated tactical purpose; inventing that geometry now would fabricate missing design. The named-space tactical requirements are preserved as acceptance context for a future Progressive Decomposer rather than resolved here.\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2899:          \"reference\": \"Section 5 - Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2900:          \"requirement\": \"The required delivery target is a Windows Standalone build; the canonical gameplay scene must be registered in Unity Build Settings before the Windows delivery requirement can be considered complete.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2905:          \"reference\": \"Section 5 - Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2932:          \"reference\": \"Section 5 - Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2933:          \"requirement\": \"The current canonical gameplay scene is Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity; that scene (or a later human-approved replacement) must be registered in Unity Build Settings before the Windows delivery requirement can be considered complete. A committed EditorBuildSettings.asset with no registered canonical gameplay scene is confirmed incomplete build configuration. The top-level Assets/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity and Assets/Scenes/SampleScene.unity stubs do not satisfy this requirement merely because they are .unity files.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2938:          \"reference\": \"Section 5 - Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2939:          \"requirement\": \"The canonical gameplay scene (currently Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity, or a later human-approved replacement) is registered in Unity Build Settings.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2942:          \"reference\": \"Section 5 - Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2952:          \"reference\": \"Section 5 - Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2966:          \"observation\": \"m_Scenes: [] — no scene is currently registered in Unity Build Settings, directly confirming the GDD's stated incomplete build-configuration fact.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2974:          \"evidence\": \"ProjectSettings/EditorBuildSettings.asset currently contains m_Scenes: [] as the sole authoritative Unity build-scene list for this project.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:2996:          \"reference\": \"Section 5 - Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:3003:      \"title\": \"No concurrent Unity asset edits\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:3009:          \"requirement\": \"Tasks that depend on unfinished work, or that require changes to the same scripts, scenes, or prefabs, are completed sequentially. Agents do not modify the same Unity assets concurrently.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:3013:          \"requirement\": \"Two agents will not modify the same gameplay files or Unity assets at the same time.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:3025:          \"requirement\": \"API credentials will remain outside the Unity project and source control.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:3089:          \"requirement\": \"Unity scenes, prefab references, animations, and Inspector assignments require human inspection. Agent-generated work is not merged until its Play Mode checklist has been tested.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:3093:          \"requirement\": \"The human developer remains responsible for architecture, merging changes, inspecting Unity scenes and prefabs, playtesting, balance, and final integration.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:3121:          \"requirement\": \"The finished Windows game will not use generative AI at runtime; enemy behavior, spell effects, doors, damage, and pursuit run locally through standard Unity systems. The game requires no external AI service, API key, token usage, or network connection after it is built.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:3137:          \"requirement\": \"Development-time art acquisition may use approved tools that can generate isometric tiles, props, and directional sprites. Those outputs are imported into Unity as normal assets; they do not imply automatic prefab creation, collision setup, sorting configuration, or gameplay integration.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/reconciliation.json:3176:      \"evidence\": \"Directly stated as a required cross-cutting process constraint in Section 4; the specific per-agent ownership boundaries (Wizard Combat, Enemy Pursuit, Door and Interaction, victory coordination, Dungeon Encounter, Unity Validation) are represented as acceptance criteria/dependencies on their respective owning work items across the other domain workers.\"\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/architecture_review.py:72:            \"Git/GitHub synchronization, concurrency assumptions, and duplicated sources \"\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/architecture_review.py:88:        \"name\": \"Unity Production Engineer\",\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/architecture_review.py:90:            \"Evaluate whether the architecture survives real Unity production: scenes, \"\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/architecture_review.py:92:            \"PlayMode/EditMode testing, batch mode, asset conflicts, worktrees, shared files, \"\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/architecture_review.py:521:from architecture documents, implementation, tests, Git history, Unity/project evidence,\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/architecture_review.py:531:tests, accepted ADR decisions, Git history, Unity/project state, task/evidence structures,\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/architecture_review.py:542:- Unity `Assets/` and tests where relevant to your specialty\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/architecture_review.py:552:1. Is the architecture fundamentally sound for autonomous Unity game development?\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/architecture_review.py:575:  covering state, planning, execution, validation, recovery, and Unity integration.\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/architecture_review.py:645:- Unity realities the synthesis abstracted away;\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/architecture_review.py:731:        \"dirty_worktree_allowed\": bool(args.allow_dirty),\nPipeline/TaskGraph/README.md:32:A conformant result does not establish dependency readiness. Dependency-readiness policy and dispatch authorization policy have not been implemented or approved. `taskcontrol ready` remains unavailable, `taskcontrol authorize` remains denied, and state inspection and a conformant result never authorize autonomous execution. Zero tasks may be autonomously dispatched. See `CONFORMANCE_RECORDS.md` and `Docs/AI-Pipeline/ADR-033_EVIDENCE_DERIVED_CONFORMANCE.md`.\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/README.md:170:This command does not derive a dependency-ready frontier. Evidence-derived current conformance has been proven on NSC-023, but a conformant result does not establish dependency readiness. Dependency-readiness and dispatch authorization policies have not been implemented or approved.\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/yagni_complexity_critic.json:8:    \"architecture_summary\": \"The architecture has a sound kernel—durable state, bounded workers, branch isolation, deterministic checks, Unity runtime evidence, bounded repair, and human design authority—but it is wrapped in a reconciliation-first control plane that has not executed a single persisted task. Direct bounded agent runs produced the door prototype, mana, and camera; subsequent effort largely hardened reconciliation and task bootstrap.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/yagni_complexity_critic.json:13:      \"Runtime Unity evidence may override a source-level evaluator PASS.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/yagni_complexity_critic.json:15:      \"Single-worker-first is appropriate for Unity scenes and shared assets.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/yagni_complexity_critic.json:24:      \"Unity compilation, tests, runtime evidence, and merge-driven completion are documented but not integrated.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/yagni_complexity_critic.json:35:      \"Progressive decomposition before an actionable oversized task has failed.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/yagni_complexity_critic.json:36:      \"Artifact Authority, promotion metadata, and multi-evaluator Artifact GER before a real missing-design artifact exists.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/yagni_complexity_critic.json:37:      \"GitHub Projects mirroring, leases, merge queues, continuous scheduling, and parallel worker arbitration.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/yagni_complexity_critic.json:51:      \"Unity batch compile/EditMode/PlayMode execution on a machine with Unity.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/yagni_complexity_critic.json:58:      \"Future GitHub status would duplicate Tasks unless one is strictly a projection.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/yagni_complexity_critic.json:60:      \"Until real generated design artifacts become common, ordinary reviewed design files and PRs provide the needed authority boundary without a separate Artifact Authority subsystem.\"\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/yagni_complexity_critic.json:69:    \"throughput_assessment\": \"Major near-term leverage is not demonstrated. Assignment 3 delivered the door prototype, Assignment 5 delivered mana, and Assignment 6 delivered the camera. From the August 18 pipeline-plan commit through the frozen commit, there are 147 first-parent commits and no later NoSafeCircle asset commit; 114 touch reconciliation-related material. Current machinery selects seven ready tasks but cannot execute one. With further infrastructure forbidden, the first break is that taskcontrol prints a task and nothing consumes it; next come unavailable automated Unity validation and manual completion state.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/yagni_complexity_critic.json:73:      \"Inspect Unity scenes, packages, build settings, visual behavior, feel, and balance.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/yagni_complexity_critic.json:75:      \"Review and merge until automated Unity evidence is trustworthy.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/yagni_complexity_critic.json:81:      \"why\": \"Mouse-directed movement is visible core gameplay and unlocks door and spell work. It forces the actual missing path—input assets, implementation, scene integration, Unity evidence, human feel testing, repair, merge, and state update. Its task file already contains enough context, while the planned RAG is stale.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/yagni_complexity_critic.json:86:        \"Run Unity compilation and focused tests on the development machine, followed by a short human playtest for feel and camera/door integration.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/yagni_complexity_critic.json:94:      \"Progressive Decomposer and Artifact Authority Gate.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/yagni_complexity_critic.json:96:      \"GitHub Projects synchronization.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/yagni_complexity_critic.json:103:      \"description\": \"Use a thin vertical loop. State: reviewed GDD/decision files with stable requirement IDs, main as implementation truth, one small task ledger with design-impact states, and ignored/external run journals. Planning: a human or one LLM drafts only the next one to three slices from the current GDD diff and repository search, applied through normal review. Execution: an explicit task ID creates an isolated branch/worktree and invokes one coding worker that plans within the task. Validation: cheap checks, Unity batch compilation/tests on the real Unity machine, then risk-based human playtesting; semantic review only where tests cannot decide. Recovery: preserve branch, logs, and evidence; permit bounded repair; then block with a concrete question. Unity changes remain serial around the canonical scene/builder. GDD changes trigger requirement-linked impact review rather than full reconciliation.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/yagni_complexity_critic.json:109:        \"observation\": \"Records two runtime camera failures after static PASS and one no-op Refiner before successful human Unity validation.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/PROPOSED_GRAPH_DELTA.md:37:| world | feature | World and Unity Foundations | open | not_applicable |  | no-safe-circle |  |\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/PROPOSED_GRAPH_DELTA.md:60:| pipeline_constraint | No concurrent Unity asset edits | confirmed | Stated identically in both Section 4 and Section 5 as a hard process invariant; applications of this rule are represented per-task via exclusive_resources across all domain workers. |\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/PROPOSED_GRAPH_DELTA.md:72:| pipeline_constraint | Development Agent Ownership Invariants | confirmed | Directly stated as a required cross-cutting process constraint in Section 4; the specific per-agent ownership boundaries (Wizard Combat, Enemy Pursuit, Door and Interaction, victory coordination, Dungeon Encounter, Unity Validation) are represented as acceptance criteria/dependencies on their respective owning work items across the other domain workers. |\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/prompts/verification/evidence_auditor.md:20:- `Packages/manifest.json` when declared Unity package availability is directly relevant\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/prompts/verification/evidence_auditor.md:21:- `Packages/packages-lock.json` when resolved/locked Unity package availability is directly relevant\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/adversarial_qa.json:8:    \"architecture_summary\": \"The architectural kernel is good: durable repository state, deterministic dependency calculation, bounded workers, isolated Git work, and evidence-based validation are the right primitives. The current architecture is nevertheless incomplete at its most important boundaries. It treats mutable task status as trusted truth, has no implemented moving-GDD synchronization path, proposes reusing a demonstrably stale RAG index, and has no general path from `taskcontrol ready` to Unity-validated merge. It should be reorganized around versioned requirements, evidence-derived completion, and one vertical gameplay execution loop—not continued in the current milestone order.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/adversarial_qa.json:18:      \"Unity scenes, prefabs, Input Actions, ProjectSettings, and builders are recognized as conflict surfaces.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/adversarial_qa.json:22:      \"False-completion sequence: edit a task's `status` to `complete`; the loader intentionally accepts it; dependents immediately become ready; no merge commit, acceptance evidence, or Unity result is required. A downstream worker can therefore build against an interface that never landed.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/adversarial_qa.json:26:      \"Completion authority is duplicated among `Tasks/*.yaml`, integrated `main`, and future GitHub Done/merge state. No transaction or derivation rule closes split-brain cases such as merge succeeding while task update fails, or task completion landing before implementation.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/adversarial_qa.json:44:      \"Artifact Authority and Artifact GER address future design-invention risks; the initial graph contains zero artifact tasks and no production artifact flow exists.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/adversarial_qa.json:45:      \"Progressive Decomposition addresses future oversized or underspecified frontier work; none is required to execute the currently ready NSC-003/004/005 tasks.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/adversarial_qa.json:46:      \"GitHub Projects mirroring, Draft PR automation, claims, leases, and continuous scheduling protect future operational failures; no supervisor currently uses them.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/adversarial_qa.json:49:      \"A general scanner and incremental cache may eventually save tokens, but targeted `rg`, Unity YAML inspection, and direct GDD extraction are enough for the next gameplay slice.\"\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/adversarial_qa.json:56:      \"Milestone 2 combines RAG, scanner, context packing, decomposition, artifact authority, generation, GER, promotion, and graph mutation. Those are multiple products, not one bounded milestone.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/adversarial_qa.json:57:      \"Assignment 6's no-op rule created a perverse incentive: pass 4 added a defensive warning/test partly to produce a diff, even though the unresolved proof needed was Unity runtime validation.\"\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/adversarial_qa.json:62:      \"Incremental GDD change-impact analysis.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/adversarial_qa.json:67:      \"Unity batch compilation/EditMode/PlayMode execution on the actual Windows/Unity host and ingestion of its results.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/adversarial_qa.json:76:      \"Necessary boundary: runtime/Unity evidence must remain independent of worker claims.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/adversarial_qa.json:79:      \"Duplicate responsibility: future GitHub Done would add a third completion indicator unless it is only a projection.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/adversarial_qa.json:94:    \"throughput_assessment\": \"Current leverage is narrow: the repository can persist a coarse backlog and calculate seven structurally ready tasks. It cannot autonomously implement, validate, recover, merge, or truthfully complete one. If forbidden to build infrastructure for one slice, the machinery first stops immediately after `taskcontrol ready` because no generic dispatcher exists. Bypassing that manually exposes stale canon/context next, and completion then blocks on unavailable Unity-host evidence and an unguarded status edit. Major leverage remains plausible, but only after a repeatable vertical ticket loop is proven; the current milestone sequence would likely delay that proof further.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/adversarial_qa.json:99:      \"Perform Unity visual/runtime inspection where automated evidence is insufficient.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/adversarial_qa.json:112:        \"Execute NSC-003 in one isolated worktree with a bounded coding worker.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/adversarial_qa.json:115:        \"Regenerate and validate the canonical scene and Input Actions on Unity 6000.1.8f1; preserve machine-readable compile/EditMode/PlayMode results.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/adversarial_qa.json:123:      \"Full Progressive Decomposer\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/adversarial_qa.json:124:      \"Artifact Authority and Artifact GER platform\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/adversarial_qa.json:127:      \"GitHub Projects synchronization\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/adversarial_qa.json:136:      \"description\": \"Replace the milestone stack with a small event-sourced ticket controller. State: split gameplay canon, technical ADRs, pipeline policy, and derived repository inventory; give requirements stable IDs/content hashes; keep Tasks as reviewed intent/topology; store claims, failures, validations, and approvals as append-only run events; derive completion/readiness from main plus matching evidence. Planning: maintain a small human-prioritized near frontier, use an LLM only for bounded decomposition, and make global reconciliation an occasional audit. Execution: one worktree, one bounded worker, and exact task/canon/base/context hashes. Validation: declarative required gates, cheap checks first, Unity batch compile/EditMode/PlayMode on the Windows host, targeted semantic review, and explicit human-only visual/feel gates. Recovery: idempotent transitions, leases, preserved commits/logs, bounded retries, and stale-run cancellation. Unity integration: explicit asset locks, builder execution, serialized scene/GUID checks, and test evidence attached to the candidate commit. GDD edits emit requirement-change events that invalidate only affected work and artifacts; graph changes remain reviewed PRs.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/adversarial_qa.json:137:      \"why_better\": \"It covers state, planning, execution, validation, recovery, and Unity while eliminating duplicate mutable completion authority. Moving canon becomes a routine, deterministic state transition instead of an occasional expensive reconciliation. Most importantly, it concentrates engineering on the shortest loop that can ship gameplay repeatedly.\"\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/adversarial_qa.json:146:        \"observation\": \"Readiness trusts only dependency status strings; no evidence, Git, canon, blocked-state, or Unity check participates.\"\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/adversarial_qa.json:174:        \"observation\": \"Two static evaluator passes preceded two Unity runtime failures; lines 150–158 document the false no-op refinement.\"\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/adversarial_qa.json:178:        \"observation\": \"Unity validation remained unavailable to the automated pass; human scene regeneration and tests were still required.\"\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/adversarial_qa.json:198:    \"biggest_uncertainty\": \"An external Windows/Unity CI runner or operational supervisor may exist outside the frozen repository. No integration, machine-readable results, or authority binding for such a system is evidenced in this commit.\"\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/TASK_CONTRACT_SCHEMA_V2.md:160:- deriving its historical camera completion gates from the recorded Unity test evidence;\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/evidence/NSC-023/artifacts/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests-86af98f41ab5.xml:7:    <test-suite type=\"Assembly\" id=\"1017\" name=\"NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.Tests.Editor.dll\" fullname=\"C:/UnityProjects/NoSafeCircleAgentCrew/NoSafeCircle/Library/ScriptAssemblies/NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.Tests.Editor.dll\" runstate=\"Runnable\" testcasecount=\"14\" result=\"Passed\" start-time=\"2026-08-22 19:54:53Z\" end-time=\"2026-08-22 19:54:54Z\" duration=\"0.387675\" total=\"12\" passed=\"12\" failed=\"0\" inconclusive=\"0\" skipped=\"0\" asserts=\"0\">\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/evidence/NSC-023/artifacts/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests-86af98f41ab5.xml:10:        <property name=\"_APPDOMAIN\" value=\"Unity Child Domain\" />\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/player_core.json:2:  \"current_state_summary\": \"Current repository state for player-core systems (Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scripts/PlayerMovement.cs, PlayerHealth.cs, PlayerMana.cs, PlayerManaUI.cs, plus their scene wiring in DoorPrototype.unity and creation code in DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs): PlayerMovement.cs is a CharacterController-driven WASD keyboard mover that polls Keyboard.current directly every Update; it has no mouse-directed click/hold movement, no cursor-to-gameplay-plane projection/world-space pointer target, no movement-restriction request/release interface, and no floor-restart reset entry point. It does call PlayerInteractionController.OnPlayerMoved() on movement input, which is the one GDD-relevant behavior already present. PlayerHealth.cs exposes CurrentHealth, a clamped TakeDamage(amount) damage interface, and a Damaged event, but has no restore/heal interface, no observable zero-health/death transition, and no reset entry point; there is no PlayerHealth UI component anywhere in the scripts, so the GDD's continuous health indicator is not implemented. PlayerMana.cs exposes CurrentMana/MaxMana, Spend(amount) (fails silently returning false when insufficient), Tick-based post-cast-delay regeneration, and a ManaSpent event; PlayerManaUI.cs already renders a continuous fill-bar tied to CurrentMana/MaxMana (confirmed wired in DoorPrototype.unity via DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.BuildManaUI). PlayerMana has no reset entry point and no denied-cast feedback signal. All four scripts (PlayerHealth, PlayerMana, PlayerMovement, PlayerManaUI) are confirmed serialized onto the Player/Canvas GameObjects in Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity (verified by matching each script's .meta GUID against the scene file), and DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.BuildPlayer/BuildUI is the current write surface that creates and wires them. Assets/InputSystem_Actions.inputactions is the unmodified default Unity template (Move/Look/Attack/Interact/Crouch/Jump/etc. plus a UI map with Point/Click); it has no gameplay-plane cursor-projection or click-to-move action, and PlayerMovement.cs does not consume it at all — it bypasses the Input Actions layer entirely via raw Keyboard.current polling, which conflicts with the GDD's explicit \\\"routed through Unity Input System/Input Actions\\\" requirement. PlayerManaPlayModeTests.cs and part of DoorInteractionPlayModeTests.cs give real Play Mode test coverage for PlayerMana spend/regen/post-cast-delay behavior and for PlayerHealth's damage-cancels-door-interaction interaction, but no tests exist for movement, cursor projection, health restore, or any reset behavior.\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/player_core.json:3:  \"desired_state_summary\": \"Per the current GDD (Docs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md), Player Movement must own player position/locomotion, movement-restriction state, and a shared cursor-to-gameplay-plane projection producing a world-space pointer target consumed by movement itself, cursor-aimed spells (Fireball, Frost Field), and Door/Interaction (Force Wave is the explicit player-centered exception). Movement must be mouse-directed (click to set a destination or hold to steer toward the cursor) and routed through the project's Unity Input System/Input Actions layer rather than direct hardware polling. Player Movement must expose an owner-controlled movement-restriction interface that Charged Fireball requests/releases without mutating movement internals directly, and must expose an owner-controlled reset entry point for player position/movement state consumed by the Floor Run/Restart Orchestrator. Player Health must remain the single owner of current health, exposing an owner-controlled damage interface (already present) plus an owner-controlled restore/heal interface clamped to max health (consumed by Door's lock-and-heal request), an observable zero-health/death transition consumed by the restart orchestrator without polling or internal mutation, a continuous player-facing health indicator, no passive regeneration, and an owner-controlled floor-restart reset entry point. Player Mana must remain the single owner of current mana and its post-cast regeneration-delay state, exposing the existing spend interface plus an owner-controlled reset entry point for floor restart, while never absorbing spell-local cooldown/charge/cast/placement/active-field state that belongs to Fireball, Frost Field, or Force Wave; denied-cast attempts should surface player-readable feedback given the GDD's failure-readability success criterion (exact presentation is an implementation choice).\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/player_core.json:6:    \"PlayerMovement bypasses the Unity Input System/Input Actions layer entirely (direct Keyboard.current polling) despite `using UnityEngine.InputSystem`, which conflicts with the GDD's explicit 'routed through the project's Unity Input System/Input Actions layer' requirement for both movement and door/spell input.\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/player_core.json:7:    \"Assets/InputSystem_Actions.inputactions is the stock Unity template asset (Move/Look/Attack/Interact/Crouch/Jump/Sprint/etc.) with no cursor-to-gameplay-plane or click-to-move action; implementing mouse-directed movement will require adding/modifying bindings in this shared asset.\",\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/player_core.json:54:          \"requirement\": \"Mouse-directed movement through the shared Unity Input System/Input Actions layer: click to set a destination or hold to keep steering toward the cursor; Player Movement owns the shared cursor-to-gameplay-plane projection and exposes the resulting world-space pointer target consumed by movement, cursor-aimed spells, and cursor-targeted interactions (Force Wave is the exception).\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/player_core.json:68:          \"requirement\": \"Movement is mouse-directed: a click sets a destination the wizard walks toward, and holding continues steering toward the current cursor position, consumed through Unity Input System/Input Actions rather than direct hardware polling.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/player_core.json:104:          \"observation\": \"Unmodified stock Unity template action asset; Player map has only WASD/Dpad Move and button actions (Attack/Interact/Crouch/Jump/etc.), no cursor-to-gameplay-plane or click-to-move action, and PlayerMovement.cs does not reference it.\"\r\nPipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5/workers/player_core.json:117:          \"evidence\": \"GDD §2 Move and Aim requires routing through Unity Input System/Input Actions; current InputSystem_Actions.inputactions has no click-to-move or cursor-projection action for the Player map.\"\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/PROJECT_REQUIREMENTS.yaml:17:          \"reference\": \"Section 5 - Approved Unity Packages and Windows Build Configuration\",\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/PROJECT_REQUIREMENTS.yaml:24:      \"title\": \"No concurrent Unity asset edits\",\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/PROJECT_REQUIREMENTS.yaml:30:          \"requirement\": \"Tasks that depend on unfinished work, or that require changes to the same scripts, scenes, or prefabs, are completed sequentially. Agents do not modify the same Unity assets concurrently.\"\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/PROJECT_REQUIREMENTS.yaml:34:          \"requirement\": \"Two agents will not modify the same gameplay files or Unity assets at the same time.\"\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/PROJECT_REQUIREMENTS.yaml:46:          \"requirement\": \"API credentials will remain outside the Unity project and source control.\"\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/PROJECT_REQUIREMENTS.yaml:110:          \"requirement\": \"Unity scenes, prefab references, animations, and Inspector assignments require human inspection. Agent-generated work is not merged until its Play Mode checklist has been tested.\"\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/PROJECT_REQUIREMENTS.yaml:114:          \"requirement\": \"The human developer remains responsible for architecture, merging changes, inspecting Unity scenes and prefabs, playtesting, balance, and final integration.\"\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/PROJECT_REQUIREMENTS.yaml:142:          \"requirement\": \"The finished Windows game will not use generative AI at runtime; enemy behavior, spell effects, doors, damage, and pursuit run locally through standard Unity systems. The game requires no external AI service, API key, token usage, or network connection after it is built.\"\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/PROJECT_REQUIREMENTS.yaml:158:          \"requirement\": \"Development-time art acquisition may use approved tools that can generate isometric tiles, props, and directional sprites. Those outputs are imported into Unity as normal assets; they do not imply automatic prefab creation, collision setup, sorting configuration, or gameplay integration.\"\r\nPipeline/TaskGraph/PROJECT_REQUIREMENTS.yaml:197:      \"evidence\": \"Directly stated as a required cross-cutting process constraint in Section 4; the specific per-agent ownership boundaries (Wizard Combat, Enemy Pursuit, Door and Interaction, victory coordination, Dungeon Encounter, Unity Validation) are represented as acceptance criteria/dependencies on their respective owning work items across the other domain workers.\"\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/workflow_systems_architect.json:10:    \"verdict_reason\": \"Do not discard the bounded-worker and evidence-validation ideas, but reject the current milestone order and durable-state design. The most important post-bootstrap transitions are absent, while the repository has already invested heavily in global reconciliation, multi-model verification, recovery, and copied audit output. This will not materially accelerate game development until one real feature can travel through planning, execution, Unity validation, merge, and durable completion.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/workflow_systems_architect.json:16:      \"Unity/runtime evidence can override a static evaluator pass.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/workflow_systems_architect.json:44:      \"Claims, leases, resource collision scheduling, worktrees, GitHub synchronization, and merge queues mostly protect future parallel execution; no production supervisor currently exercises them.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/workflow_systems_architect.json:45:      \"Artifact Authority and Artifact GER may eventually prevent unauthorized design invention, but the current graph contains zero artifact records and no real feature has needed this path.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/workflow_systems_architect.json:46:      \"Progressive Decomposition is plausible for distant content work, but building it before one ready task ships would protect a hypothetical planning problem while execution remains impossible.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/workflow_systems_architect.json:49:      \"Broad GitHub Projects mirroring, automatic backlog replenishment, parallel workers, and merge queues have no near-term proof of value.\"\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/workflow_systems_architect.json:66:      \"A real Unity batch adapter that captures compilation, EditMode, and PlayMode results in machine-readable form on a Unity-capable machine.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/workflow_systems_architect.json:70:      \"A defined one-way GitHub projection or a deliberate choice to make GitHub the operational authority; the current future mirror has no conflict protocol.\"\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/workflow_systems_architect.json:76:      \"`Tasks/*.yaml` is called the operational authority while GitHub is planned to own transient status, but no ordering or reconciliation rule handles disagreement, network failure, crash recovery, or a merged PR with an open task.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/workflow_systems_architect.json:86:      \"The current Unity implementation and PlayMode tests still encode release-cancels-held-interaction behavior invalidated by the newer GDD. This demonstrates that passing tests may certify obsolete canon.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/workflow_systems_architect.json:90:      \"RAG indexes and context packs must be content-addressed by GDD revision and fail closed on mismatch. Cache invalidation cannot remain a two-line future note.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/workflow_systems_architect.json:91:      \"Synchronization with evolving canon should be incremental requirement-impact analysis, not repeated global roadmap reconstruction.\"\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/workflow_systems_architect.json:93:    \"throughput_assessment\": \"The present system is unlikely to turn weeks into days. It proves that a large initial plan can be audited and queried, but not that gameplay can be delivered repeatedly. If forbidden to build infrastructure for one slice, an agent could manually implement NSC-003 from its task file, but the machinery would first risk supplying stale RAG canon and would later have no authoritative way to claim the task, resume after interruption, validate it in Unity, associate evidence with a merge, or mark it complete. The first hard workflow break is the missing run/completion transaction; the first correctness break is stale canon retrieval. The absence of any `Assets/` commit after Assignment 6 while roughly 130k lines were added elsewhere is strong negative throughput evidence.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/workflow_systems_architect.json:97:      \"Perform Unity editor inspection of scenes, prefabs, serialized references, imports, lighting, and visual composition.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/workflow_systems_architect.json:100:      \"Resolve Unity scene/prefab/shared-asset conflicts and decide the fate of non-canonical stub scenes.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/workflow_systems_architect.json:102:      \"Supply credentials, Unity licensing/environment access, and external assets without committing secrets.\"\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/workflow_systems_architect.json:112:        \"Run Unity compilation plus focused EditMode/PlayMode tests on a Unity-capable machine and record a human Play Mode checklist/result for movement feel and pointer correctness.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/workflow_systems_architect.json:116:      \"proof_of_value\": \"From a clean restart, one NSC-003 attempt resumes without duplicate work, produces playable mouse movement on the canonical scene, passes recorded Unity validation, merges once, and changes the durable materialized state exactly once so NSC-019 becomes ready. That is the first credible evidence of development leverage.\"\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/workflow_systems_architect.json:120:      \"Broad Progressive Decomposition infrastructure.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/workflow_systems_architect.json:121:      \"Artifact Authority, artifact generation, or Artifact GER until a real blocked feature needs new approved design.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/workflow_systems_architect.json:123:      \"GitHub Projects bidirectional synchronization; first choose one operational authority.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/workflow_systems_architect.json:132:      \"description\": \"Replace the reconciliation-first roadmap platform with a thin, change-driven feature-delivery controller. State: keep the GDD and a small stable requirement registry in Git; make task-contract revisions immutable and bind each to requirement/GDD hashes; keep exactly one mutable workflow ledger with explicit states (`planned`, `ready`, `running`, `validating`, `review`, `blocked`, `needs_human`, `done`, `superseded`, `needs_replan`). Treat GitHub as a one-way projection unless deliberately selected as that ledger. Planning: create or revise only near-frontier vertical slices from requirement changes and current repository evidence; no global multi-model reconciliation during normal progress. Execution: the controller performs a compare-and-set claim, creates/reuses a deterministic branch/worktree, records base SHA and attempt identity, then launches one bounded coding worker. Validation: collect Git scope checks, compilation, Unity EditMode/PlayMode XML, task-specific semantic review, and explicit human gates into one evidence manifest. Completion: a merged PR/merge SHA is implementation authority; recovery detects merged-but-unclosed tasks and records completion exactly once. Recovery: replay the ledger, inspect branch/worktree/PR/merge state, and resume or escalate without trusting worker output. Unity integration: run a small machine-local Unity CLI adapter and retain human Play Mode/editor checks for visual or interaction claims. GDD evolution: compute requirement-level changes, mark linked planned/active work as impacted, invalidate stale contexts/evidence, and create remediation/revalidation work for previously completed implementations without rewriting history.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/workflow_systems_architect.json:182:        \"observation\": \"Static evaluation passed twice while the camera remained unusable; human Unity testing and structured runtime feedback drove the successful repairs.\"\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/reviews/workflow_systems_architect.json:210:    \"biggest_uncertainty\": \"Unity runtime execution and GitHub operational state may exist outside the frozen repository. Nothing in commit `aacbd57e10c9b4e9e40ef356935877527bc2411e` demonstrates such an external supervisor, lifecycle ledger, CI adapter, or synchronization protocol.\"\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/adversarial_critique.json:9:      \"The “next slice” is another platform bundle: NSC-003 implementation, contract redesign, Input Actions, scene/builder changes, new input/scene tests, a Unity host runner, restartable coordination, completion receipts, four authority-negative tests, and telemetry. Failure would not reveal whether the feature, Unity lane, or new controller was responsible.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/adversarial_critique.json:17:      \"Unity is not a thin subprocess detail. There is no repository-owned runner or CI and no Unity executable in the review shell. The current ignored `Library/` is 1.3 GB. Unity documents that a batch process cannot open a project already open in the Editor, fresh projects need explicit target selection, and `-quit` can terminate `-runTests` prematurely; its Asset Database is a per-project import cache that can be costly to regenerate. These constraints need an explicit host policy, not an assumed command wrapper. See [Unity 6.1 command-line arguments](https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.1/Documentation/Manual/EditorCommandLineArguments.html) and [Asset Database contents](https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.1/Documentation/Manual/asset-database-contents.html).\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/adversarial_critique.json:19:      \"False editable completion is real but currently dormant: no autonomous dispatcher exists and a human owns merge. Building receipt infrastructure before proving the Unity host and one manual feature repeats the project’s infrastructure-displacement pattern. Since Assignment 6, 201 commits added 130,935 lines across 379 files with zero changes under Assets, Packages, or ProjectSettings.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/adversarial_critique.json:20:      \"The one-ticket-per-day and 60–90-minute human target has no baseline. Prior camera work required two runtime failures, three refinements, a no-op, and manual Unity testing. Moreover, the deterministic pipeline is not one green executable gate: standard unittest discovery finds zero TaskGraph tests, and `deterministic_finding_identity_smoke_test.py` fails at the frozen commit.\"\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/adversarial_critique.json:22:    \"strongest_counter_case\": \"The synthesis correctly rejects the bundled Milestone 2, but then repeats its central error under the name “evidence-bound slice”: it combines a foundational, design-ambiguous gameplay rewrite with first-time workflow, Unity, recovery, authority, and measurement machinery. With one worker, human merge authority, and no automatic dispatcher, the immediate production risk is not forged task completion; it is spending the remaining window building another controller before proving that the project can compile, test, build, and ship a small change. A safer architecture is deliberately manual and serial for two tickets: human-approved frozen contract, whole current GDD, ordinary branch/checkpoint commits, one coding worker, Unity validation on the real host, human playtest, merge, and a simple delivery log containing the merge SHA and evidence paths. `taskcontrol` remains advisory and cannot auto-dispatch. GDD edits do not globally stop an active contract; the human explicitly continues, supersedes, or cancels it at the next checkpoint. Only a repeated measured bottleneck earns automation. If autonomous dispatch is later required, build a real lifecycle/conformance ledger with revert and crash semantics rather than a receipt bolt-on.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/adversarial_critique.json:30:      \"A licensed Unity 6000.1.8f1 Windows host, required platform modules, project-lock discipline, import-cache policy, and artifact-return path already exist.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/adversarial_critique.json:38:      \"The Unity review asked to prove the actual Windows build lane and define Library/import, licensing, project-lock, target-platform, and serialization behavior. The synthesis reduced this to a thin compile/test command and waived the build.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/adversarial_critique.json:44:      \"The remaining development window is consumed by coordinator, receipt, scene-test refactoring, and Unity-host plumbing before mouse movement becomes playable.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/adversarial_critique.json:48:      \"Fresh-worktree imports, project locks, target switching, or batch-test behavior exhaust repair budgets and are misdiagnosed as agent implementation failures.\",\r\nPipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/adversarial_critique.json:53:    \"better_next_step_if_any\": \"Freeze new architecture work for a two-ticket production experiment. First, on the actual Windows Unity 6000.1.8f1 host in a disposable checkout, establish a clean compile/EditMode/PlayMode baseline, execute ready task NSC-037 to register only the canonical scene, and produce a Windows development build; record commands, import time, human minutes, logs, XML, and all working-tree mutations manually. Second, deliver a narrowed NSC-005 through one ordinary branch because it extends one existing component and existing PlayMode tests without Input Actions or scene-builder changes. Use the whole current GDD, checkpoint commits, human Unity validation, merge, and a manually recorded merge SHA; do not auto-dispatch dependents. Repeat this flow once before writing a coordinator. Before attempting NSC-003, require a one-page human-approved controls decision covering button mapping, UI/door/spell priority, approach arrival/cancellation, movement pathing, and restriction ownership.\",\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
